/* Generated by spec/build_hints.py. */
window.HINTS = {"2023B-p1#c": ["<p>The claim concerns every integer assignment, not a fixed finite grid. Start with the polynomial's finite total degree and choose a sufficiently large finite set of integers.</p>", "<p>Schwartz–Zippel: a nonzero polynomial of total degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, evaluated at independent uniform coordinates from a finite field subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, is zero with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. A zero evaluation alone does not prove polynomial identity.</p>", "<p>Apply Schwartz–Zippel on a grid whose side length exceeds that degree. If the polynomial vanished at every grid point, its zero probability would be one, contradicting the bound.</p>", "<p>Name the polynomial and field, bound degree, choose the evaluation set, and check the error direction. Distinguish a formal zero polynomial from a function vanishing on a finite domain.</p>", "<p>Mention the underlying characteristic-zero field and total degree. A polynomial can vanish on a small finite grid without being the zero polynomial.</p>"], "2024C-p1#b": ["<p>Compare the size of the proposed coordinate set with the total degree. You need existence of one nonzero evaluation, not nonzeroness at every point.</p>", "<p>Schwartz–Zippel: a nonzero polynomial of total degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, evaluated at independent uniform coordinates from a finite field subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, is zero with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. A zero evaluation alone does not prove polynomial identity.</p>", "<p>Sample every coordinate independently from that set. The zero-probability bound is strictly below one, so some grid point must have nonzero value.</p>", "<p>Name the polynomial and field, bound degree, choose the evaluation set, and check the error direction. Distinguish a formal zero polynomial from a function vanishing on a finite domain.</p>", "<p>State why probability strictly below one gives existence. Check that the listed field elements are distinct in the underlying field.</p>"], "2025B-p1#a": ["<p>This is a univariate polynomial question. Count how many distinct roots are being demanded relative to its degree.</p>", "<p>Schwartz–Zippel: a nonzero polynomial of total degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, evaluated at independent uniform coordinates from a finite field subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, is zero with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. A zero evaluation alone does not prove polynomial identity.</p>", "<p>A nonzero univariate degree-d polynomial has at most d distinct roots. Verify the number of distinct roots of unity under the question's field assumptions, then compare.</p>", "<p>Name the polynomial and field, bound degree, choose the evaluation set, and check the error direction. Distinguish a formal zero polynomial from a function vanishing on a finite domain.</p>", "<p>Do not confuse ‘roots of the equation’ with only primitive roots of exactly that order. Use the meaning intended by the statement.</p>"], "2024A-p6#x": ["<p>Write a general member of the span as a linear combination with symbolic coefficients, then take its determinant.</p>", "<p>A determinant is a signed sum of products. Variables can encode combinatorial objects as monomials, but cancellation must be addressed. A nonzero total-degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial vanishes at independent uniform coordinates in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>If the span contains an invertible matrix, this determinant polynomial is not identically zero. Its total degree is at most the matrix dimension. Evaluate a random combination and test invertibility.</p>", "<p>Define the polynomial, identify its terms and justify noncancellation. Bound degree and sampling range. Explain evaluation, recovery if required, error direction and runtime.</p>", "<p>Prove both cases and the one-sided error direction. Include the cost of forming the combination and computing its determinant.</p>"], "2025A-p5#x": ["<p>Name the difference of the two symbolic determinants as one polynomial. Equality becomes a zero-polynomial question.</p>", "<p>Schwartz–Zippel: a nonzero polynomial of total degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, evaluated at independent uniform coordinates from a finite field subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, is zero with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. A zero evaluation alone does not prove polynomial identity.</p>", "<p>Bound the determinant difference's degree by the matrix dimension. Assign shared edge variables consistently in both graphs, evaluate both determinants, and compare their values.</p>", "<p>Name the polynomial and field, bound degree, choose the evaluation set, and check the error direction. Distinguish a formal zero polynomial from a function vanishing on a finite domain.</p>", "<p>Using different random values for the same variable in the two determinants would invalidate the test. State the failure probability and arithmetic or bit-cost model.</p>"], "2025C-p4#x": ["<p>For each branching program, express the accepted-path sum as the designated entry of its layer-matrix product. Subtract the two outputs.</p>", "<p>Schwartz–Zippel: a nonzero polynomial of total degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, evaluated at independent uniform coordinates from a finite field subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, is zero with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. A zero evaluation alone does not prove polynomial identity.</p>", "<p>Bound degree using the number of layers and label degrees. Evaluate the same random variable assignment in both programs and multiply the numerical matrices instead of expanding all paths.</p>", "<p>Name the polynomial and field, bound degree, choose the evaluation set, and check the error direction. Distinguish a formal zero polynomial from a function vanishing on a finite domain.</p>", "<p>Explain why the matrix entry equals the program polynomial and why evaluation is polynomial time. Bound error using total degree, not the number of paths.</p>"], "2023A-p5#x": ["<p>Suppose the best completion has rank r. Then some r-by-r minor is nonzero under that completion; fix that minor for the analysis.</p>", "<p>A determinant is a signed sum of products. Variables can encode combinatorial objects as monomials, but cancellation must be addressed. A nonzero total-degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial vanishes at independent uniform coordinates in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Its symbolic determinant is a nonzero polynomial of degree at most r. Randomly fill the unknown entries, compute rank, and bound the chance this fixed witness minor becomes zero.</p>", "<p>Define the polynomial, identify its terms and justify noncancellation. Bound degree and sampling range. Explain evaluation, recovery if required, error direction and runtime.</p>", "<p>You need not enumerate all minors or union bound over them. Prove the returned rank cannot exceed the optimum, and account for repeated variables if entries are linked.</p>"], "hw2026s1-p2#x": ["<p>Begin with leaves and the problem's exact convention for formula size. The statement must be compatible with a single variable leaf.</p>", "<p>Addition takes at most the maximum child degree; multiplication takes at most their sum. A formula is a tree, whereas a circuit can reuse subcomputations.</p>", "<p>At an addition node, use the larger child degree. At a multiplication node, add child degrees and compare with the size of the parent tree.</p>", "<p>Induct on the formula tree. Handle leaves, addition and multiplication, comparing both degree and size with the corresponding child quantities.</p>", "<p>Treat constants and the zero polynomial consistently. Do not reuse this argument for circuits with shared subexpressions without changing the size analysis.</p>"], "hw2026s1-p1#x": ["<p>Induct on the number of variables. Regard the polynomial as a univariate polynomial in the last variable whose coefficients are polynomials in the others.</p>", "<p>Schwartz–Zippel: a nonzero polynomial of total degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, evaluated at independent uniform coordinates from a finite field subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, is zero with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. A zero evaluation alone does not prove polynomial identity.</p>", "<p>Separate the event that its leading coefficient vanishes from the event that the resulting nonzero univariate polynomial vanishes. The coefficient's degree plus the last-variable degree is at most the total degree.</p>", "<p>Name the polynomial and field, bound degree, choose the evaluation set, and check the error direction. Distinguish a formal zero polynomial from a function vanishing on a finite domain.</p>", "<p>Do not apply the univariate root bound when every specialized coefficient is zero. Handle that event with the inductive hypothesis and then combine the bounds.</p>"], "hw2026s1-p4#x": ["<p>Parameterize the matrix span by one variable per input matrix. Invertibility is detected by the determinant of that symbolic combination.</p>", "<p>A determinant is a signed sum of products. Variables can encode combinatorial objects as monomials, but cancellation must be addressed. A nonzero total-degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial vanishes at independent uniform coordinates in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Prove that a single invertible combination makes the determinant polynomial nonzero. Randomly choose coefficients from a sufficiently large field subset and check the resulting numerical rank.</p>", "<p>Define the polynomial, identify its terms and justify noncancellation. Bound degree and sampling range. Explain evaluation, recovery if required, error direction and runtime.</p>", "<p>Give the sampling-set size, error direction and cost of constructing the matrix. A zero determinant on one sample is not a certain ‘no’ certificate.</p>"], "hw2026s1-p5#x": ["<p>Place distinct variables on a diagonal matrix between the two given matrices in a product whose determinant is defined.</p>", "<p>A determinant is a signed sum of products. Variables can encode combinatorial objects as monomials, but cancellation must be addressed. A nonzero total-degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial vanishes at independent uniform coordinates in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Expand by Cauchy–Binet. Each size-n index set gives a distinct squarefree monomial with coefficient equal to the product of its two minors. Nonzero coefficients correspond exactly to common bases.</p>", "<p>Define the polynomial, identify its terms and justify noncancellation. Bound degree and sampling range. Explain evaluation, recovery if required, error direction and runtime.</p>", "<p>Check dimensions and transpose placement. Distinct monomials avoid cancellation between different index sets; nonzero minors on both sides are necessary.</p>"], "hw2025s1-p4#x": ["<p>Choose one fingerprinting approach and define exactly what Alice sends. A polynomial fingerprint lets a bit string become coefficients of a univariate polynomial.</p>", "<p>Schwartz–Zippel: a nonzero polynomial of total degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, evaluated at independent uniform coordinates from a finite field subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, is zero with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. A zero evaluation alone does not prove polynomial identity.</p>", "<p>Use a shared or transmitted random evaluation point in a field of size larger than the degree by the needed error factor. Bob evaluates his string at the same point and compares fingerprints.</p>", "<p>Name the polynomial and field, bound degree, choose the evaluation set, and check the error direction. Distinguish a formal zero polynomial from a function vanishing on a finite domain.</p>", "<p>Count bits for both the evaluation point and field value, specify how the field is agreed upon, and prove equal strings never disagree while unequal ones collide only with the bounded probability.</p>"], "2025C-p1#a": ["<p>Translate being a root of unity into an equation and write the larger exponent as an integer multiple of the smaller one.</p>", "<p>A k-th root of unity satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></math>. A primitive k-th root has exact multiplicative order k; that is stronger than merely satisfying the equation.</p>", "<p>Raise the smaller-power identity to the quotient exponent. If the wording means exact multiplicative order, distinguish dividing the larger order from having exactly that larger order.</p>", "<p>Translate the definitions into powers, substitute the divisibility relation, and conclude the appropriate root-of-unity statement. Distinguish primitive roots from all roots.</p>", "<p>State which meaning of ‘order-k root’ is being used. Being a root of the larger unity equation does not imply being primitive of the larger order.</p>"], "2023A-p6#x": ["<p>Build two coefficient arrays with a one at each input-set element and zero elsewhere. Ask what the coefficient at a target sum counts.</p>", "<p>Polynomial multiplication computes convolution: the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. FFT multiplication costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations when padded length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> exceeds the product degree.</p>", "<p>Multiply the two polynomials. Every pair contributes once at the exponent equal to its sum; positive coefficients identify the sumset. Choose padding beyond the maximum possible sum.</p>", "<p>Define the encoding, prove what coefficients represent, choose enough padding, multiply and decode. Bound degrees, arithmetic cost and any required coefficient sizes.</p>", "<p>Explain both membership and representation counts. Runtime depends on the numeric universe bound, not just the number of elements in the sets.</p>"], "2024B-p2#x": ["<p>Split the input vector in half and write the two output halves using the four blocks of the Hadamard matrix.</p>", "<p>Block recursion can reduce a transform to two half-size transforms plus linear-time sums and differences. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The recursion index need not equal vector length.</p>", "<p>Compute the two smaller transforms once each, then obtain the output halves by their sum and difference. Do not recursively recompute a transform for every block occurrence.</p>", "<p>Split the vector, expand the block product, reuse recursive results, give the base case, and solve the recurrence in the actual dimension.</p>", "<p>Use vector dimension in the recurrence and translate from the matrix's index if necessary. Include the scalar base case.</p>"], "2025A-p1#b": ["<p>Track the degree of each intermediate power, not just the number of multiplications. The final coefficient vector can be much longer than the original one.</p>", "<p>Polynomial multiplication computes convolution: the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. FFT multiplication costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations when padded length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> exceeds the product degree.</p>", "<p>Use repeated squaring with FFT products. Their lengths grow geometrically, so summing their actual costs can avoid an unnecessary extra logarithmic factor.</p>", "<p>Define the encoding, prove what coefficients represent, choose enough padding, multiply and decode. Bound degrees, arithmetic cost and any required coefficient sizes.</p>", "<p>State the degree of the input polynomial from the question and the arithmetic-cost model. Do not price every product at the smallest input size.</p>"], "2025B-p4#x": ["<p>Evaluation changes polynomial multiplication into pointwise multiplication. Locate the assumption in the question that the divisor does not vanish at the chosen roots of unity.</p>", "<p>Polynomial multiplication computes convolution: the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. FFT multiplication costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations when padded length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> exceeds the product degree.</p>", "<p>Evaluate both polynomials by FFT at those roots, divide corresponding values to get quotient values, then apply the inverse FFT. The stated quotient degree bound makes these values sufficient for unique recovery.</p>", "<p>Define the encoding, prove what coefficients represent, choose enough padding, multiply and decode. Bound degrees, arithmetic cost and any required coefficient sizes.</p>", "<p>Cite the nonvanishing assumption before dividing. Give the quotient degree bound and count the two evaluations, pointwise divisions and interpolation in the runtime.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p5#x": ["<p>Multiply the stated block matrix by a vector split into two halves. Write the resulting pair of vector expressions.</p>", "<p>Block recursion can reduce a transform to two half-size transforms plus linear-time sums and differences. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The recursion index need not equal vector length.</p>", "<p>Reuse two recursive transforms and combine them by elementwise addition and subtraction. The number of entries touched at this level is linear in the vector length.</p>", "<p>Split the vector, expand the block product, reuse recursive results, give the base case, and solve the recurrence in the actual dimension.</p>", "<p>Check signs in both output halves, the base case, and whether the matrix index denotes dimension or its logarithm.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p2#a": ["<p>You already have the two direct products. Expand the product of the two sums of halves and locate the two cross terms.</p>", "<p>Recover a cross term using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Three half-size products yield <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; addition and shifts still cost work.</p>", "<p>Subtract the known direct products from this one extra multiplication. The remainder is exactly the cross-term sum needed for recombination.</p>", "<p>Split inputs, compute the two direct products and the product of sums, recover the cross term, recombine, and solve the recurrence.</p>", "<p>Count multiplications separately from additions. Do not compute either cross product individually after obtaining their sum.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p2#b": ["<p>Use part (a)'s multiplication count as the number of recursive children. Identify the work spent on additions and recombination.</p>", "<p>Separate recursive work from work in the current call. A recursion tree sums costs across levels. If level costs shrink by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, their sum is at most the root cost divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>The recurrence has three half-size calls and linear local work. Compare the linear exponent with the exponent determined by three children and a halving factor.</p>", "<p>Write the recurrence and base case. Bound each level's work, sum it and account for leaves. Give a matching lower bound when a tight bound is requested.</p>", "<p>State a constant-size base case and include carries if this is integer multiplication. The exponent comes from the number of recursive products, not the number of cross terms.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p1#x": ["<p>After multiplication, determine the largest exponent of the first variable. The substitution base must exceed that value so exponent pairs cannot collide.</p>", "<p>Polynomial multiplication computes convolution: the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. FFT multiplication costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations when padded length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> exceeds the product degree.</p>", "<p>Encode an exponent pair as a mixed-radix integer exponent using the stated substitution. Multiply the resulting univariate polynomials, then recover each pair by quotient and remainder.</p>", "<p>Define the encoding, prove what coefficients represent, choose enough padding, multiply and decode. Bound degrees, arithmetic cost and any required coefficient sizes.</p>", "<p>Check collision-freedom for product degrees, not only input degrees. Bound the encoded degree and decoding cost.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p3#x": ["<p>Place indicator coefficients at elements of the two sets. Write one coefficient of their product as a sum over index pairs.</p>", "<p>Polynomial multiplication computes convolution: the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. FFT multiplication costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations when padded length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> exceeds the product degree.</p>", "<p>Every product of two one-coefficients corresponds to a representation of that exponent as a sum. Read all requested counts from one FFT product.</p>", "<p>Define the encoding, prove what coefficients represent, choose enough padding, multiply and decode. Bound degrees, arithmetic cost and any required coefficient sizes.</p>", "<p>Specify the universe size, sufficient padding and exact integer recovery if discussing numerical FFT implementation. Presence and number of representations are different outputs.</p>"], "2014A-p6#x": ["<p>Split each binary integer into high and low halves using a power of two as the base. Expand the product symbolically.</p>", "<p>Recover a cross term using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Three half-size products yield <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; addition and shifts still cost work.</p>", "<p>Replace the two cross multiplications by a product of sums minus the direct products. Recombine using shifts and additions, then write the resulting recurrence.</p>", "<p>Split inputs, compute the two direct products and the product of sums, recover the cross term, recombine, and solve the recurrence.</p>", "<p>Sums of halves can gain one bit; mention why this does not change the exponent. Count bit operations for additions and shifts under the required model.</p>"], "2023A-p1#d": ["<p>Think of each four-by-four matrix as a two-by-two matrix of two-by-two blocks.</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>Apply Strassen at the block level, then again inside each small block product. Multiply the number of block products by the scalar multiplications per block.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>The question counts multiplications, not all arithmetic operations. Distinguish a block multiplication from a scalar multiplication.</p>"], "2024A-p1#b": ["<p>A nonnegative determinant constrains the product of eigenvalues. Could two negative eigenvalues have a positive product?</p>", "<p>For real symmetric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, PSD means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every real vector. The spectral theorem gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>&#x0039B;</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math>. The determinant only records the product of eigenvalues, not their separate signs.</p>", "<p>Try a small diagonal symmetric matrix with two negative diagonal entries. Compute both its determinant and its quadratic form at a coordinate vector.</p>", "<p>Use the exact PSD definition or diagonalization. For a counterexample, verify hypotheses and a negative quadratic form. For a factorization, check dimensions and multiply back.</p>", "<p>Verify symmetry and the determinant hypothesis, then exhibit a negative quadratic form. An unexplained matrix example is not a complete counterexample.</p>"], "2024A-p1#c": ["<p>Ask how to extract a linearly independent spanning subset before orthogonalizing it.</p>", "<p>Gram–Schmidt subtracts a vector's projections onto previously constructed orthonormal vectors, then normalizes the residual. A zero residual signals linear dependence and must be skipped. Gaussian elimination can first extract a basis.</p>", "<p>Apply Gaussian elimination or incremental independence tests, then Gram–Schmidt on the independent vectors. Skip zero residuals rather than dividing by their norm.</p>", "<p>Extract independent spanning vectors, describe projection subtraction and normalization, prove orthogonality and preservation of span, and count operations under the specified model.</p>", "<p>Handle dependent input vectors and distinguish arithmetic operations from exact representation of square roots if the complexity model asks for bit lengths.</p>"], "2024C-p2#x": ["<p>Compare one Boolean product entry with the corresponding ordinary integer product entry for zero–one inputs.</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>The integer entry counts witnesses and lies between zero and the matrix dimension. Replace positive entries by one after a fast integer product.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>Use integer arithmetic or a modulus too large for positive counts to become zero. Multiplication modulo two computes parity, not Boolean OR.</p>"], "2025A-p1#c": ["<p>Place all the input vectors side by side as columns of a single matrix. What are the columns of its product with the given matrix?</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>Compute the whole collection of matrix–vector outputs by one fast matrix–matrix multiplication, then extract columns.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>Include construction and output costs and compare the resulting exponent with three. Independent naive matrix–vector computations are not a lower bound.</p>"], "2025B-p1#b": ["<p>Partition the wide right-hand matrix into square blocks of the same width as the left-hand matrix.</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>Multiply the left matrix by each square block and concatenate the outputs. Count the number of square products and multiply by their fast-multiplication cost.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>Check dimensions and the output-size cost. Explain why the final exponent is strictly below the naive one.</p>"], "2023B-p5#x": ["<p>Add self-loops so a walk of shorter length can be padded to the power you compute. Interpret positive entries of powers.</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>A reachable vertex has a simple path of length at most one less than the vertex count. Use repeated Boolean squaring, thresholding after products, to capture all such paths without huge path counts.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>Prove both directions of reachability and state how the zero-length diagonal convention is handled. Include the logarithmic number of matrix products.</p>"], "2024B-p1#d": ["<p>Relate elementary symmetric functions of eigenvalues to coefficients of the characteristic polynomial. Check the requested index.</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>Expand the formal product of the linear factors using eigenvalues. The coefficient corresponding to the requested degree gives the answer up to a known sign; the product of all eigenvalues is the determinant.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>Track sign and dimension carefully. Do not assume the eigenvalues must first be computed numerically.</p>"], "2024B-p4#x": ["<p>For a candidate distance, express ‘every ordered pair has a path of at most this length’ using a power of the adjacency matrix with self-loops.</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>The predicate is monotone in the candidate distance. Test it using Boolean matrix exponentiation and binary search within the simple-path bound.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>Use the strong-connectivity assumption for a finite search range. Count products per test and number of tests; prevent integer path-count blowup by Boolean thresholding.</p>"], "2025C-p1#b": ["<p>Partition each large matrix into a square array of smaller blocks. Apply the smaller multiplication scheme at the block level.</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>Each block product can itself use the same smaller scheme, so the multiplication counts multiply. Justify that the scheme's additions and scalar coefficients remain valid on blocks.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>Check the model of multiplication algorithms: a scalar procedure using divisions or commutativity cannot automatically be lifted to noncommuting blocks. State the bilinear/block-compatible assumption used.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p4#x": ["<p>Use self-loops to make exact-length powers represent bounded-length reachability. Begin from the adjacency relation rather than enumerating paths.</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>Repeatedly square in the Boolean sense until the represented length covers every simple path. Each Boolean product can use an ordinary nonnegative product followed by thresholding.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>Bound both the number of squarings and entry magnitudes after thresholding. Explain why a positive entry cannot represent a nonexistent path.</p>"], "hw2025s2-p4#x": ["<p>Expand a diagonal entry of the cube of the adjacency matrix. It counts closed walks of length three starting at that vertex.</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>For a simple undirected loopless graph, identify how many starting vertices and orientations correspond to one triangle. Sum the diagonal entries and divide by this multiplicity.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>State the graph assumptions and multiplicity explicitly. Count the matrix multiplications and the final trace computation.</p>"], "hw2025s2-p5#x": ["<p>Write the effect of adding the identity to the adjacency matrix. A path can now be padded by staying at a vertex.</p>", "<p>Square matrix multiplication takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Compatible rectangular products can be split into square blocks. Repeated squaring needs logarithmically many products.</p>", "<p>Raise this relation to a sufficiently large power by repeated squaring. Interpret nonzero entries and use Boolean thresholding to keep intermediate counts controlled.</p>", "<p>Specify dimensions and what products or powers encode. Describe blocking or exponentiation, count all work, and distinguish arithmetic complexity from bit complexity.</p>", "<p>Justify the exponent from the longest needed simple path, and count all matrix multiplications. Clarify whether diagonal reachability is included.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p5#a": ["<p>Prove equality of kernels first rather than comparing ranks directly. One inclusion follows immediately by multiplying by the transpose.</p>", "<p>Over the reals, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> have the same kernel. Apply rank–nullity. A symmetric matrix is diagonalizable, so zero-eigenvalue multiplicity equals kernel dimension.</p>", "<p>For the other inclusion, multiply the equation involving the Gram matrix on the left by the vector's transpose. A squared real norm equal to zero forces the original matrix–vector product to vanish.</p>", "<p>State dimensions and field. Prove the kernel or eigenvalue claim, use rank–nullity, and explain the computation. For parallel algorithms, bound depth and work separately.</p>", "<p>Both linear maps have the same domain, so rank–nullity applies. The real-field assumption matters to the squared-norm argument.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p5#b": ["<p>Diagonalize the symmetric matrix and ask how many diagonal coordinates can map nontrivially.</p>", "<p>Over the reals, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> have the same kernel. Apply rank–nullity. A symmetric matrix is diagonalizable, so zero-eigenvalue multiplicity equals kernel dimension.</p>", "<p>Similarity by an invertible orthogonal matrix preserves rank. The diagonal matrix's rank counts its nonzero eigenvalues, including multiplicities.</p>", "<p>State dimensions and field. Prove the kernel or eigenvalue claim, use rank–nullity, and explain the computation. For parallel algorithms, bound depth and work separately.</p>", "<p>Distinguish algebraic from geometric multiplicity and state why symmetry makes them equal. Use the actual matrix dimension in the subtraction.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p5#c": ["<p>Combine the previous kernel result with the characteristic polynomial of the Gram matrix. Which coefficient reveals the multiplicity of the zero root?</p>", "<p>Over the reals, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> have the same kernel. Apply rank–nullity. A symmetric matrix is diagonalizable, so zero-eigenvalue multiplicity equals kernel dimension.</p>", "<p>Compute that polynomial using the permitted parallel routine, then find the smallest exponent with a nonzero coefficient. Subtract the zero-root multiplicity from the column dimension.</p>", "<p>State dimensions and field. Prove the kernel or eigenvalue claim, use rank–nullity, and explain the computation. For parallel algorithms, bound depth and work separately.</p>", "<p>Give parallel depth and processor/work bounds for matrix formation, characteristic polynomial and coefficient selection. Handle the all-zero matrix.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p6#a": ["<p>Take an eigenvector and insert it into the defining quadratic-form inequality.</p>", "<p>For real symmetric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, PSD means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every real vector. The spectral theorem gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>&#x0039B;</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math>. The determinant only records the product of eigenvalues, not their separate signs.</p>", "<p>Use the eigenvector equation to rewrite the quadratic form as the eigenvalue times a strictly positive squared norm. Infer the sign of the eigenvalue.</p>", "<p>Use the exact PSD definition or diagonalization. For a counterexample, verify hypotheses and a negative quadratic form. For a factorization, check dimensions and multiply back.</p>", "<p>State real symmetry so the eigenvalues and eigenvectors in this argument have the required properties. The eigenvector must be nonzero.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p6#b": ["<p>Write an orthogonal diagonalization and use part (a) to take real square roots of its diagonal eigenvalues.</p>", "<p>For real symmetric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, PSD means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every real vector. The spectral theorem gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>&#x0039B;</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math>. The determinant only records the product of eigenvalues, not their separate signs.</p>", "<p>Choose a factor formed from the square-root diagonal matrix and the transpose of the eigenvector matrix. Multiply its transpose by itself to verify the factorization.</p>", "<p>Use the exact PSD definition or diagonalization. For a counterexample, verify hypotheses and a negative quadratic form. For a factorization, check dimensions and multiply back.</p>", "<p>Check the order of factors; reversing them can give the wrong matrix. Zero eigenvalues are allowed and do not require inverses.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p6#c": ["<p>Substitute the factorization into an arbitrary quadratic form.</p>", "<p>For real symmetric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, PSD means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every real vector. The spectral theorem gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>&#x0039B;</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math>. The determinant only records the product of eigenvalues, not their separate signs.</p>", "<p>Reassociate the expression as the squared norm of the factor times the vector. This is nonnegative for every real vector; also check symmetry of the product.</p>", "<p>Use the exact PSD definition or diagonalization. For a counterexample, verify hypotheses and a negative quadratic form. For a factorization, check dimensions and multiply back.</p>", "<p>Prove the claim for every vector, not only basis vectors. The factor need not be invertible or square.</p>"], "2018A-p1#a": ["<p>Identify Strassen's number of recursive products, child dimension, and matrix-addition work.</p>", "<p>Separate recursive work from work in the current call. A recursion tree sums costs across levels. If level costs shrink by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, their sum is at most the root cost divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Write seven half-size recursive calls plus quadratic local work. Compare the resulting master-theorem exponent with two.</p>", "<p>Write the recurrence and base case. Bound each level's work, sum it and account for leaves. Give a matching lower bound when a tight bound is requested.</p>", "<p>Include a base case and report the tight bound under the stated arithmetic model. Do not confuse Strassen's seven products with classical multiplication's eight.</p>"], "2024C-p1#d": ["<p>Compare the determinant expansion with the expression counting perfect matchings. The only apparent difference is the permutation sign.</p>", "<p>A determinant is a signed sum of products. Variables can encode combinatorial objects as monomials, but cancellation must be addressed. A nonzero total-degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial vanishes at independent uniform coordinates in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Work in characteristic two, where minus one and plus one coincide. Each nonzero permutation term corresponds to one perfect matching, so the determinant gives the count's parity.</p>", "<p>Define the polynomial, identify its terms and justify noncancellation. Bound degree and sampling range. Explain evaluation, recovery if required, error direction and runtime.</p>", "<p>Use the bipartite adjacency matrix and compute over the field with two elements. A zero determinant here means even parity, not necessarily absence of a perfect matching.</p>"], "2023A-p3#x": ["<p>Randomly assign each vertex to one of three parts and define an indicator that an edge crosses between parts.</p>", "<p>The current conditional expectation is an average of expectations for the next possible choices. At least one choice does not decrease it. These expectations must be efficiently computable for a deterministic algorithm.</p>", "<p>Compute one edge's crossing probability, then derandomize by placing vertices sequentially where the conditional expected final crossing count is largest. Express this choice using already placed neighbours.</p>", "<p>Analyse the random construction, describe deterministic choices, prove the conditional-expectation invariant, then conclude with the final score, comparison to optimum and runtime.</p>", "<p>Prove the invariant and efficient computation, then compare with the upper bound of all edges on the optimum. An expected random guarantee alone does not supply the requested deterministic algorithm.</p>"], "2023B-p3#x": ["<p>Find a collection of pairwise disjoint hyperedges by repeatedly taking an uncovered hyperedge.</p>", "<p>An approximation needs a feasible output and a comparison with optimum. Charge optimal objects or necessary cost to algorithm choices, with bounded charges per choice. The ratio direction differs for minimization and maximization.</p>", "<p>Select all three vertices of each chosen hyperedge. Every vertex cover must pay at least one vertex for each disjoint chosen hyperedge, while your algorithm pays three.</p>", "<p>Describe the algorithm, prove feasibility, identify the optimum bound, prove charging multiplicity, derive the ratio in the correct direction, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Show the process covers every hyperedge at termination and that chosen witness hyperedges are disjoint. This unweighted argument does not automatically handle arbitrary vertex costs.</p>"], "2024B-p5#x": ["<p>Write the weighted hitting-set relaxation with one variable per ground element and one covering constraint per required subset.</p>", "<p>A minimization LP lower-bounds the integer optimum. If at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> nonnegative terms sum to at least one, one is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>. Rounding up at that threshold preserves covering and increases cost by at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Round up every variable at least the reciprocal of the maximum subset size. For a fixed subset, not all its fractional variables can lie below this threshold.</p>", "<p>State the relaxation and threshold. Prove feasibility per constraint, bound each selected cost by fractional cost, sum, compare to the integer optimum, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Prove coverage and a weighted cost bound separately. The parameter is the size of a required subset, not the number of subsets containing an element.</p>"], "2025B-p2#b": ["<p>Use the weighted set-cover LP from part (a). Here frequency bounds the number of set variables in any one element's constraint.</p>", "<p>A minimization LP lower-bounds the integer optimum. If at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> nonnegative terms sum to at least one, one is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>. Rounding up at that threshold preserves covering and increases cost by at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Select every set whose fractional variable is at least the reciprocal of the frequency. Bound the selected set's cost by frequency times its fractional contribution.</p>", "<p>State the relaxation and threshold. Prove feasibility per constraint, bound each selected cost by fractional cost, sum, compare to the integer optimum, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Include equality at the rounding threshold. State the lower-bound relation between LP optimum and integer optimum before concluding the approximation ratio.</p>"], "2025C-p2#b": ["<p>Fix an arbitrary element and look at a feasible optimal primal covering solution together with the optimal dual.</p>", "<p>Complementary slackness pairs each positive optimal primal variable with a tight dual constraint. A tight set's cost is the sum of its element prices. A feasible dual value lower-bounds the integral covering optimum.</p>", "<p>The primal coverage constraint implies some containing set has a positive primal variable. Complementary slackness then makes that set's dual inequality tight.</p>", "<p>Use an optimal primal solution to show tight sets cover. Sum their costs as dual prices, interchange sums, bound how often each price is counted, and compare with optimum.</p>", "<p>The dual must be optimal, not merely feasible. Show this for every element to conclude that all tight sets form a cover.</p>"], "2025C-p2#c": ["<p>Start from the feasible tight-set cover established in part (b), and write its total cost as a sum over selected sets.</p>", "<p>Complementary slackness pairs each positive optimal primal variable with a tight dual constraint. A tight set's cost is the sum of its element prices. A feasible dual value lower-bounds the integral covering optimum.</p>", "<p>Replace each tight set's cost by its sum of dual element prices. Swap summations; each element's price is counted no more than its frequency.</p>", "<p>Use an optimal primal solution to show tight sets cover. Sum their costs as dual prices, interchange sums, bound how often each price is counted, and compare with optimum.</p>", "<p>Use nonnegative dual prices and weak duality to compare with the integer optimum. Do not count only one occurrence of an element that belongs to several selected sets.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p6#a": ["<p>Choose a nonzero eigenvector and apply the PSD inequality to that particular vector.</p>", "<p>For real symmetric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, PSD means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every real vector. The spectral theorem gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>&#x0039B;</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math>. The determinant only records the product of eigenvalues, not their separate signs.</p>", "<p>The quadratic form becomes the eigenvalue times the vector's positive squared norm. Divide by that norm to infer the eigenvalue's sign.</p>", "<p>Use the exact PSD definition or diagonalization. For a counterexample, verify hypotheses and a negative quadratic form. For a factorization, check dimensions and multiply back.</p>", "<p>Use real symmetry and nonzero eigenvectors. Checking diagonal entries alone would not prove the eigenvalue claim.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p6#b": ["<p>Diagonalize the symmetric matrix orthogonally. Its nonnegative eigenvalues have real square roots, including zero.</p>", "<p>For real symmetric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, PSD means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every real vector. The spectral theorem gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>&#x0039B;</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math>. The determinant only records the product of eigenvalues, not their separate signs.</p>", "<p>Form a factor from the diagonal square roots and the transposed orthogonal matrix, then multiply its transpose by itself.</p>", "<p>Use the exact PSD definition or diagonalization. For a counterexample, verify hypotheses and a negative quadratic form. For a factorization, check dimensions and multiply back.</p>", "<p>Write the factor explicitly and verify multiplication order. The desired factorization is transpose-times-factor, not necessarily the reverse.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p6#c": ["<p>Take an arbitrary real vector and insert the proposed factorization into its quadratic form.</p>", "<p>For real symmetric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, PSD means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every real vector. The spectral theorem gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>&#x0039B;</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></math>. The determinant only records the product of eigenvalues, not their separate signs.</p>", "<p>The result is a squared Euclidean norm, so it is nonnegative. Also observe that a transpose-times-factor product is symmetric.</p>", "<p>Use the exact PSD definition or diagonalization. For a counterexample, verify hypotheses and a negative quadratic form. For a factorization, check dimensions and multiply back.</p>", "<p>Quantify over every real vector. Neither invertibility nor positive definiteness is required.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p3#a": ["<p>Fix a nonempty vertex subset and try spreading total vertex mass one uniformly over it.</p>", "<p>Uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> includes weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>. Shared-threshold events can depend on one another; linearity still holds. A ratio of expectations is not the expectation of a ratio.</p>", "<p>Set vertex weights to its reciprocal size on that subset and zero elsewhere, with the same weight on its internal edges. Verify the LP rows and calculate its objective.</p>", "<p>Define the threshold sets, check weight ranges, compute expected numerator and denominator using indicators, and average their difference rather than incorrectly averaging their ratio.</p>", "<p>Match the question's density convention, including any factor of two. A feasible LP point for each subset proves the relaxation upper-bounds the maximum density.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p3#b": ["<p>For a fixed feasible vertex-weight vector, inspect the largest allowed value of each edge variable.</p>", "<p>Uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> includes weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>. Shared-threshold events can depend on one another; linearity still holds. A ratio of expectations is not the expectation of a ratio.</p>", "<p>Both endpoint constraints cap it by the smaller endpoint weight. Replacing it by that minimum preserves the upper bounds and can only increase an objective with positive edge coefficients.</p>", "<p>Define the threshold sets, check weight ranges, compute expected numerator and denominator using indicators, and average their difference rather than incorrectly averaging their ratio.</p>", "<p>Check all other constraints before claiming feasibility, and keep the vertex weights unchanged. This is a WLOG statement about a maximization LP.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p3#c": ["<p>Use the threshold definitions and the minimum-endpoint identity from part (b) for one fixed edge.</p>", "<p>Uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> includes weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>. Shared-threshold events can depend on one another; linearity still holds. A ratio of expectations is not the expectation of a ratio.</p>", "<p>Both endpoints pass a threshold exactly when their minimum weight passes it. This is precisely the edge-threshold condition.</p>", "<p>Define the threshold sets, check weight ranges, compute expected numerator and denominator using indicators, and average their difference rather than incorrectly averaging their ratio.</p>", "<p>State that the equivalence is for an existing graph edge. Two surviving vertices do not create an edge absent from the original graph.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p3#f": ["<p>Subtract the LP objective times the threshold vertex count from the threshold edge count. Use parts (d) and (e) to examine its expectation.</p>", "<p>Uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> includes weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>. Shared-threshold events can depend on one another; linearity still holds. A ratio of expectations is not the expectation of a ratio.</p>", "<p>With a normalized optimal vertex-weight total, the difference has mean zero, so a nonempty threshold set must attain nonnegative difference. Justify nonemptiness using positive threshold intervals or the finite list of positive weight breakpoints.</p>", "<p>Define the threshold sets, check weight ranges, compute expected numerator and denominator using indicators, and average their difference rather than incorrectly averaging their ratio.</p>", "<p>Do not use an empty set to satisfy a vacuous inequality, and do not replace the expectation of a ratio by a ratio of expectations. Explain how to find a suitable breakpoint.</p>"], "hw2026s4-p3#b": ["<p>Let one indicator record whether each set is selected by randomized rounding.</p>", "<p>An indicator is 1 when its event happens and 0 otherwise. Its expectation is the probability of that event. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, without an independence requirement.</p>", "<p>Each indicator's expectation is its selection probability, the LP value. Sum these expectations and compare the LP objective with the optimum integer cover cost.</p>", "<p>Define the random experiment and one indicator. Express the count as a sum, calculate one event's probability, sum expectations, and state the conclusion.</p>", "<p>This is an expected size bound, not a proof that the sampled family covers all elements or always has small size.</p>"], "hw2026s4-p3#c": ["<p>Fix one element and list all sets that contain it. Its failure event is that none is selected.</p>", "<p>Choose each set independently with its LP value as probability. Missing one element is a product of failure probabilities, bounded using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Independent rounds and a union bound control all elements.</p>", "<p>Multiply the independent nonselection probabilities, bound each by an exponential, and use the LP constraint that their fractional coverage totals at least one.</p>", "<p>Describe selections and rounds, bound one element's failure and then all failures, and bound expected cost separately. Round repetition counts up; expected cost is not a high-probability cost guarantee.</p>", "<p>Independence is between set selections, not between different elements' coverage events. Take the complement only after bounding noncoverage.</p>"], "hw2026s4-p3#d": ["<p>Distinguish the requested error parameter from Euler's number in the printed notation. Begin with one element's failure over repeated independent rounds.</p>", "<p>Choose each set independently with its LP value as probability. Missing one element is a product of failure probabilities, bounded using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Independent rounds and a union bound control all elements.</p>", "<p>Raise its one-round failure bound to the number of rounds, then union bound over elements. Bound the size of the union by the sum of round sizes and use linearity for its expectation.</p>", "<p>Describe selections and rounds, bound one element's failure and then all failures, and bound expected cost separately. Round repetition counts up; expected cost is not a high-probability cost guarantee.</p>", "<p>Round the number of rounds up and reflect that ceiling in the cost bound. A high-probability cover and expected size bound do not automatically give both guarantees simultaneously at the same constant.</p>"], "hw2026s4-p4#x": ["<p>Encode Boolean labels as signs. Write a satisfied equality and a satisfied inequality as expressions involving their sign product.</p>", "<p>Relax binary signs into unit vectors, replacing products with inner products. A random hyperplane separates two vectors with probability equal to their angle divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>; equal-label constraints use the complementary probability.</p>", "<p>Replace signs by unit vectors. Under a random hyperplane, use angle-over-pi for unequal labels and its complement for equal labels, then compare each with its relaxed contribution using the given approximation inequality.</p>", "<p>Write the sign objective and vector or PSD relaxation, show the optimum bound, analyse one rounded constraint, apply the scalar inequality, and sum expectations.</p>", "<p>Check both equation types, objective weights and the relaxation's bound direction. State that the approximation is in expectation unless you add the required amplification or derandomization.</p>"], "hw2025s5-p2#x": ["<p>Select a vertex, add it to the independent set, and delete it together with its remaining neighbours.</p>", "<p>An approximation needs a feasible output and a comparison with optimum. Charge optimal objects or necessary cost to algorithm choices, with bounded charges per choice. The ratio direction differs for minimization and maximization.</p>", "<p>Each chosen vertex removes at most one plus the maximum degree vertices. Charge removed vertices to that choice and compare total vertices with any optimum independent set.</p>", "<p>Describe the algorithm, prove feasibility, identify the optimum bound, prove charging multiplicity, derive the ratio in the correct direction, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Prove independence and account for every vertex exactly once in the deletion process. State the maximization ratio in the direction used by the course.</p>"], "2017A-p1#e": ["<p>Hold the accuracy parameter fixed before deciding whether the displayed runtime is polynomial in input size.</p>", "<p>A PTAS is polynomial in input size for each fixed positive accuracy parameter. An FPTAS must additionally be polynomial in reciprocal accuracy.</p>", "<p>For any fixed positive accuracy, the displayed exponent is a constant. Then separately examine what happens when reciprocal accuracy is treated as an input parameter.</p>", "<p>Check the approximation guarantee, hold accuracy fixed when inspecting input-size complexity, then separately check dependence on reciprocal accuracy.</p>", "<p>Check the approximation guarantee's direction for the optimization problem. Do not require a PTAS to be fully polynomial in reciprocal accuracy.</p>"], "2014A-p5#x": ["<p>Introduce an artificial vertex connected at zero cost to every sender. Which terminals must a connecting structure contain?</p>", "<p>A Steiner tree connects a specified terminal set and may use other vertices. A cost-preserving reduction lets an approximation for this problem transfer to another connection problem. Artificial zero-cost edges can express free links, but must be removed correctly afterward.</p>", "<p>Use all receivers and the artificial vertex as Steiner terminals, apply the known Steiner approximation, and remove the artificial edges afterward.</p>", "<p>Define the augmented graph and terminals, map feasible solutions in both directions without increasing cost, apply the known approximation, remove artificial structure, and prove the resulting feasibility and ratio.</p>", "<p>Prove optimum costs correspond in both directions and that each remaining receiver component contains a sender. Nonnegative edge costs justify pruning unnecessary cycles or components.</p>"], "2014A-p8#a": ["<p>Give every variable an independent fair truth value and one indicator per satisfied clause.</p>", "<p>On a finite probability space some outcome is at least the expectation, and some is at most it. This proves existence, not success on every random trial. Choose the direction matching the claim.</p>", "<p>For a clause of three distinct variables, calculate the probability all its literals are false and take the complement. Sum satisfaction expectations and compare with the number of clauses as an optimum upper bound.</p>", "<p>Give a random construction, express its score using indicators, compute the expectation, and apply averaging in the requested direction.</p>", "<p>Check the clause assumptions and state that the guarantee is in expectation. Overlapping clauses do not prevent linearity.</p>"], "2014A-p9#a": ["<p>Take any maximal matching and compare it with an optimum matching. What would an optimum edge disjoint from all selected endpoints imply?</p>", "<p>An approximation needs a feasible output and a comparison with optimum. Charge optimal objects or necessary cost to algorithm choices, with bounded charges per choice. The ratio direction differs for minimization and maximization.</p>", "<p>Every optimum edge touches a selected matching edge. Charge it there; each selected edge has two endpoints, and an optimum matching uses each endpoint at most once.</p>", "<p>Describe the algorithm, prove feasibility, identify the optimum bound, prove charging multiplicity, derive the ratio in the correct direction, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Use maximality only to prove coverage by endpoints. Maximal and maximum are different, and the charging multiplicity gives the factor.</p>"], "2014B-p7#b": ["<p>Inspect the last job placed on the machine that ends with maximum load under the least-loaded rule.</p>", "<p>Compare total load with capacity, and inspect the least-loaded bin or the moment a new bin opens. A volume lower bound alone does not prove a feasible packing.</p>", "<p>Before that placement, its load is at most the average load already placed. Bound the optimum using both the average total load and the largest item, then compare the final makespan.</p>", "<p>Specify the placement rule, identify its decisive event, derive a load inequality, compare with an optimum lower bound, and handle the final or exceptional bin.</p>", "<p>The objective is minimum capacity with a fixed number of bins, not minimum bin count. Identify which optimum lower bound pays for each term.</p>"], "2015B-p3#x": ["<p>Take an optimum tour and consider alternating edges as candidate matchings. Check whether the vertex count is even or odd.</p>", "<p>Tours, cycle covers and spanning trees differ in connectivity and degrees. Account separately for removed and added edges. Shortcutting is cost-safe only when the triangle inequality is available.</p>", "<p>For an even tour, the two alternating perfect matchings split the tour cost. For odd size, use an averaging argument over maximum-cardinality alternating matchings obtained by leaving one vertex out.</p>", "<p>Describe each construction step, prove the final connectivity and degree conditions, bound added and removed costs against valid optimum lower bounds, then derive the ratio.</p>", "<p>Compare with a minimum-weight maximum-cardinality matching, not an arbitrary maximal matching. State the parity case and why a candidate of the required cardinality exists.</p>"], "2016A-p5#x": ["<p>Trace the exact greedy rule on a small graph instead of importing a known guarantee for a different max-cut greedy algorithm.</p>", "<p>One instance meeting all hypotheses but violating the conclusion disproves a universal claim. For LP-rounding claims, check both feasibility and any optimality requirement before rounding.</p>", "<p>Look for an early local choice that leaves many later edges uncut. Compute both the greedy cut and a concrete better cut; enlarge or adjust the instance if the desired ratio violation is not strict.</p>", "<p>Specify the instance, verify hypotheses, calculate the relevant output, and exhibit the violated constraint or ratio. Address ties if they affect the construction.</p>", "<p>Specify vertex order and tie-breaking if the rule permits them. A bad cut alone is insufficient unless the given algorithm actually produces it.</p>"], "2017B-p2#a": ["<p>Use a slightly better value-to-weight ratio to force greedy to choose an item that blocks a much more valuable item.</p>", "<p>One instance meeting all hypotheses but violating the conclusion disproves a universal claim. For LP-rounding claims, check both feasibility and any optimality requirement before rounding.</p>", "<p>Try capacity one, a tiny item with ratio just above a full-capacity item's ratio, and a full-capacity item of much larger total value. Parameterize the tiny size to make the value ratio arbitrarily bad.</p>", "<p>Specify the instance, verify hypotheses, calculate the relevant output, and exhibit the violated constraint or ratio. Address ties if they affect the construction.</p>", "<p>Keep both items individually feasible and make the ordering strict. Check whether the algorithm compares against the best single item; that modified algorithm is a different claim.</p>"], "2018A-p2#a": ["<p>Find a required subset not yet hit, and consider selecting all of its at most three elements.</p>", "<p>An approximation needs a feasible output and a comparison with optimum. Charge optimal objects or necessary cost to algorithm choices, with bounded charges per choice. The ratio direction differs for minimization and maximization.</p>", "<p>Repeat until every subset is hit. The chosen witness subsets must be disjoint from earlier selected elements, which will supply a lower bound for the analysis.</p>", "<p>Describe the algorithm, prove feasibility, identify the optimum bound, prove charging multiplicity, derive the ratio in the correct direction, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Describe a polynomial implementation and handle an empty required subset as infeasible. Selecting only one arbitrary element does not give the same proof.</p>"], "2018A-p2#b": ["<p>Use the witness subsets selected by the algorithm in part (a), not every subset in the input.</p>", "<p>An approximation needs a feasible output and a comparison with optimum. Charge optimal objects or necessary cost to algorithm choices, with bounded charges per choice. The ratio direction differs for minimization and maximization.</p>", "<p>They are pairwise disjoint, so any hitting set needs at least one distinct element for each. Your algorithm pays at most three elements per witness subset.</p>", "<p>Describe the algorithm, prove feasibility, identify the optimum bound, prove charging multiplicity, derive the ratio in the correct direction, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Prove disjointness from the rule and then derive the cost comparison. Do not assume all input subsets are disjoint.</p>"], "2018A-p2#c": ["<p>When a new subset arrives, do nothing if it is already hit; otherwise select all its elements.</p>", "<p>An approximation needs a feasible output and a comparison with optimum. Charge optimal objects or necessary cost to algorithm choices, with bounded charges per choice. The ratio direction differs for minimization and maximization.</p>", "<p>The newly unhit witness subsets are disjoint from everything already selected. Compare their number with an offline optimum that must hit all of them.</p>", "<p>Describe the algorithm, prove feasibility, identify the optimum bound, prove charging multiplicity, derive the ratio in the correct direction, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Selections must be irrevocable and based only on arrivals so far. The lower bound may use the final offline optimum without giving the online algorithm future knowledge.</p>"], "2018B-p2#a": ["<p>To refute exact optimality, you only need a graph where a permitted minimum-degree first choice leads to a smaller independent set than another choice.</p>", "<p>One instance meeting all hypotheses but violating the conclusion disproves a universal claim. For LP-rounding claims, check both feasibility and any optimality requirement before rounding.</p>", "<p>Try joining local gadgets so one low-degree vertex blocks several vertices that could coexist. Trace every deletion and compare with an explicitly listed larger independent set.</p>", "<p>Specify the instance, verify hypotheses, calculate the relevant output, and exhibit the violated constraint or ratio. Address ties if they affect the construction.</p>", "<p>Verify the greedy choice really has minimum current degree at each step. Address ties explicitly; do not claim the optimum merely from an unproved guess.</p>"], "2018B-p2#b": ["<p>Each greedy choice deletes itself and all its current neighbours. Bound the size of this deleted group using the original maximum degree.</p>", "<p>An approximation needs a feasible output and a comparison with optimum. Charge optimal objects or necessary cost to algorithm choices, with bounded charges per choice. The ratio direction differs for minimization and maximization.</p>", "<p>These groups partition the vertices, so the number chosen is at least total vertices divided by one plus the maximum degree. Compare with the trivial upper bound on an optimum independent set.</p>", "<p>Describe the algorithm, prove feasibility, identify the optimum bound, prove charging multiplicity, derive the ratio in the correct direction, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Prove the selected vertices are independent and the algorithm runs in polynomial time. Minimum-degree selection is allowed but not needed for this coarse bound.</p>"], "2021A-p2#4": ["<p>The student-selection LP uses upper-capacity constraints. Rounding several positive fractional selections upward can overload one course.</p>", "<p>One instance meeting all hypotheses but violating the conclusion disproves a universal claim. For LP-rounding claims, check both feasibility and any optimality requirement before rounding.</p>", "<p>Construct an instance and an optimal fractional solution with multiple variables at or above the stated threshold in one capacity row. Compute the rounded row sum explicitly.</p>", "<p>Specify the instance, verify hypotheses, calculate the relevant output, and exhibit the violated constraint or ratio. Address ties if they affect the construction.</p>", "<p>Prove the fractional solution is optimal if the statement requires optimal input, and use the exact threshold parameter defined in the question. Feasibility of a fractional point alone may not refute the claim.</p>"], "2021A-p3#1": ["<p>Start from the given minimum cycle cover. Turn each cycle into a path by removing one edge.</p>", "<p>Tours, cycle covers and spanning trees differ in connectivity and degrees. Account separately for removed and added edges. Shortcutting is cost-safe only when the triangle inequality is available.</p>", "<p>Order the paths and connect the end of each to the start of the next, including a final connection back to the first. Completeness supplies every connecting edge.</p>", "<p>Describe each construction step, prove the final connectivity and degree conditions, bound added and removed costs against valid optimum lower bounds, then derive the ratio.</p>", "<p>If there is already one cycle, keep it. Explain why every vertex appears exactly once in the final tour and why all computations are polynomial.</p>"], "2021A-p3#2": ["<p>Compare the cost of the patched tour with the original cycle cover one removed-and-added edge pair at a time.</p>", "<p>Tours, cycle covers and spanning trees differ in connectivity and degrees. Account separately for removed and added edges. Shortcutting is cost-safe only when the triangle inequality is available.</p>", "<p>Each replacement adds at most one net cost because edges have weights one or two. Bound the number of cycles by one third of the vertex count, and bound optimum below by both the minimum cover cost and the vertex count.</p>", "<p>Describe each construction step, prove the final connectivity and degree conditions, bound added and removed costs against valid optimum lower bounds, then derive the ratio.</p>", "<p>The cycle-count bound assumes cycles have at least three vertices. Check the undirected simple-cycle convention; allowing two-cycles would change this proof.</p>"], "2021B-p1#4": ["<p>Focus on a capacity-one vertex incident to several positive fractional edge variables.</p>", "<p>One instance meeting all hypotheses but violating the conclusion disproves a universal claim. For LP-rounding claims, check both feasibility and any optimality requirement before rounding.</p>", "<p>Look for an optimal fractional solution with two incident variables at the rounding threshold, so rounding both up exceeds that vertex's capacity. Verify the other-side capacities as well.</p>", "<p>Specify the instance, verify hypotheses, calculate the relevant output, and exhibit the violated constraint or ratio. Address ties if they affect the construction.</p>", "<p>Show fractional optimality and use the stated non-strict threshold. An integral optimal solution may exist even though another optimal fractional solution rounds badly.</p>"], "2021B-p3#1": ["<p>Process vertices in a fixed order and count already assigned neighbours of each colour.</p>", "<p>The current conditional expectation is an average of expectations for the next possible choices. At least one choice does not decrease it. These expectations must be efficiently computable for a deterministic algorithm.</p>", "<p>Assign the new vertex the colour with the fewest already assigned neighbours. This makes at most one third of its backward incident edges monochromatic.</p>", "<p>Analyse the random construction, describe deterministic choices, prove the conditional-expectation invariant, then conclude with the final score, comparison to optimum and runtime.</p>", "<p>Explain how ties are handled and why the rule is polynomial. Only count edges to previously processed vertices at each step.</p>"], "2021B-p3#2": ["<p>Charge each edge when its later endpoint is coloured. At that moment, split backward neighbours into three colour classes.</p>", "<p>An approximation needs a feasible output and a comparison with optimum. Charge optimal objects or necessary cost to algorithm choices, with bounded charges per choice. The ratio direction differs for minimization and maximization.</p>", "<p>The least populated class contains at most one third of those neighbours, so at least two thirds of backward edges become properly coloured. Sum over vertices and compare with the optimum's maximum possible edge count.</p>", "<p>Describe the algorithm, prove feasibility, identify the optimum bound, prove charging multiplicity, derive the ratio in the correct direction, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Count every edge exactly once and preserve the fraction direction for maximization. The algorithm need not produce a proper colouring of the whole graph.</p>"], "2015A-p6#a": ["<p>At this stage, group bins by the reciprocal-integer size of their most recently placed items. Use the descending size order.</p>", "<p>Compare total load with capacity, and inspect the least-loaded bin or the moment a new bin opens. A volume lower bound alone does not prove a feasible packing.</p>", "<p>For any fixed denominator, packing items of that size fills bins in exact increments and can leave at most one unfinished bin of that type under the stated placement rule. Count the possible denominators processed so far.</p>", "<p>Specify the placement rule, identify its decisive event, derive a load inequality, compare with an optimum lower bound, and handle the final or exceptional bin.</p>", "<p>Use the exact packing rule from the question, not an arbitrary heuristic. Handle already full bins separately from the unfinished ones.</p>"], "2015A-p6#b": ["<p>Look at the next item that could not fit in a non-last bin. Its size bounds the unused space left behind.</p>", "<p>Compare total load with capacity, and inspect the least-loaded bin or the moment a new bin opens. A volume lower bound alone does not prove a feasible packing.</p>", "<p>Use the reciprocal-size ordering and the stage parameter to turn ‘does not fit’ into a lower bound on the load already in that bin.</p>", "<p>Specify the placement rule, identify its decisive event, derive a load inequality, compare with an optimum lower bound, and handle the final or exceptional bin.</p>", "<p>Track strict versus weak inequalities and why the last bin is exceptional. State which item sizes have already been processed.</p>"], "2015A-p6#c": ["<p>Try to show the total weight in all bins is greater than the number of bins minus one.</p>", "<p>Compare total load with capacity, and inspect the least-loaded bin or the moment a new bin opens. A volume lower bound alone does not prove a feasible packing.</p>", "<p>Combine the unfinished-bin count from part (a) with the load bounds from part (b), accounting for the exceptional final bin. The total item weight is a lower bound on the optimum bin count.</p>", "<p>Specify the placement rule, identify its decisive event, derive a load inequality, compare with an optimum lower bound, and handle the final or exceptional bin.</p>", "<p>An additive one guarantee is different from a multiplicative approximation ratio. Give the final integer rounding argument and handle a single-bin packing.</p>"], "2017A-p3#x": ["<p>Adding a matching on all but two odd-degree tree vertices should leave exactly two odd vertices. Check connectivity and parity first.</p>", "<p>Tours, cycle covers and spanning trees differ in connectivity and degrees. Account separately for removed and added edges. Shortcutting is cost-safe only when the triangle inequality is available.</p>", "<p>Order the tree's odd vertices along an optimal Hamilton path. Construct two candidate matchings, each leaving two of them unmatched, whose total shortcut-edge cost is at most the optimum path cost. The minimum such matching costs at most the cheaper candidate.</p>", "<p>Describe each construction step, prove the final connectivity and degree conditions, bound added and removed costs against valid optimum lower bounds, then derive the ratio.</p>", "<p>Use the triangle inequality, the MST lower bound, and the free-endpoint path setting. Prove the final Euler path can be shortcut without increasing cost; fixed prescribed endpoints would be a different problem.</p>"], "2023A-p1#a": ["<p>Distinguish moving to a different basis from moving to a different vertex. Then ask whether the stated pivot rule permits a zero reduced-cost direction.</p>", "<p>For basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, set nonbasic coordinates to zero and solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Express basics and the objective in nonbasic variables. The entering rule chooses improvement; the eligible minimum-ratio test preserves nonnegativity.</p>", "<p>Equal objective values can occur along an optimal edge if zero-cost pivots are allowed. A degenerate pivot can change the basis while leaving the point unchanged. Check which situation the exact statement requires.</p>", "<p>Write the dictionary, show the entering choice and eligible ratios, identify the leaving variable, update the point, verify feasibility, and recompute the objective.</p>", "<p>Your example must meet the pivot rule and genuinely change the vertex if requested. Degeneracy alone does not show movement to a different vertex.</p>"], "2023B-p1#b": ["<p>The question concerns one pivot, not the total number of pivots. List the linear algebra and ratio tests within one step.</p>", "<p>For basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, set nonbasic coordinates to zero and solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Express basics and the objective in nonbasic variables. The entering rule chooses improvement; the eligible minimum-ratio test preserves nonnegativity.</p>", "<p>Bound the dimension of the basis system and the arithmetic work to compute reduced costs, a pivot column and ratios. For input-length complexity, use rational basis-solution size bounds as well.</p>", "<p>Write the dictionary, show the entering choice and eligible ratios, identify the leaving variable, update the point, verify feasibility, and recompute the objective.</p>", "<p>Do not conclude simplex is polynomial overall from a polynomial pivot cost. If the claim concerns bit complexity, arithmetic-operation counts alone are insufficient.</p>"], "2024B-p1#b": ["<p>First check the proposed point satisfies the standard-form constraints. Identify the columns corresponding to strictly positive coordinates.</p>", "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, a feasible point is a vertex precisely when the columns in its positive support are linearly independent. Degeneracy can give several bases for one vertex.</p>", "<p>Test linear independence of those columns by Gaussian elimination. Explain why dependence supplies a small feasible movement in both directions, whereas independence prevents such a movement.</p>", "<p>Check feasibility, identify positive support, and test its column rank. For dependence, construct a nonzero two-sided feasible direction. Separate vertex, basis and optimum claims.</p>", "<p>Check feasibility and rank, not just the number of nonzero coordinates. Include polynomial bit complexity if the input is rational.</p>"], "2024C-p1#a": ["<p>Associate each vertex with its positive support, a subset of the coordinate indices.</p>", "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, a feasible point is a vertex precisely when the columns in its positive support are linearly independent. Degeneracy can give several bases for one vertex.</p>", "<p>For a fixed independent support, the equality system has at most one solution supported there. Count all possible supports rather than all choices of real coordinates.</p>", "<p>Check feasibility, identify positive support, and test its column rank. For dependence, construct a nonzero two-sided feasible direction. Separate vertex, basis and optimum claims.</p>", "<p>Justify uniqueness for one support. Degeneracy does not invalidate the upper bound, but counting bases as distinct vertices would overcount.</p>"], "2025C-p1#d": ["<p>An ‘if and only if’ contains two implications. Test whether every feasible vertex must optimize an arbitrary linear objective.</p>", "<p>One instance meeting all hypotheses but violating the conclusion disproves a universal claim. For LP-rounding claims, check both feasibility and any optimality requirement before rounding.</p>", "<p>A line segment with a nonconstant objective has a nonoptimal endpoint. A constant objective on the same segment also makes interior points optimal. These test the two implications separately.</p>", "<p>Specify the instance, verify hypotheses, calculate the relevant output, and exhibit the violated constraint or ratio. Address ties if they affect the construction.</p>", "<p>State a concrete feasible set and objective for your counterexample. Do not confuse ‘an optimal vertex exists’ with ‘every optimum is a vertex’.</p>"], "2023A-p4#a": ["<p>Start with one variable per edge and one capacity constraint per vertex. Relax edge-selection variables before introducing slacks.</p>", "<p>Standard form here is equality constraints and nonnegative variables. Add nonnegative slack to a ‘less than’ row, subtract it from a ‘greater than’ row, and split a free variable into the difference of two nonnegative ones.</p>", "<p>Replace integrality by nonnegativity and needed upper bounds. Add a slack to each at-most-one incidence constraint, and write the objective using only original edge variables.</p>", "<p>Handle variable signs first, convert each row, then present objective, equalities and nonnegativity together. Explain how the transformed solution represents the original one.</p>", "<p>Check the matching degree constraints, slack signs and objective direction. Explain why any omitted upper bound follows from incident vertex constraints.</p>"], "2024A-p1#d": ["<p>Clarify that strict positivity concerns nonbasic reduced costs: basic reduced costs are zero by definition.</p>", "<p>For standard-form minimization, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. Every feasible point has cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Basic reduced costs are zero; nonnegative nonbasic costs prove optimality.</p>", "<p>Compare an arbitrary feasible point's cost with the basic point. Equality forces every nonbasic coordinate to be zero; the invertible basis equations then determine the basic coordinates uniquely.</p>", "<p>Fix the sign convention, derive the cost difference from an arbitrary feasible point, use nonnegativity for optimality, and inspect equality separately for uniqueness.</p>", "<p>Distinguish a unique optimal point from a unique optimal basis. Use the minimization/maximization sign convention of the question.</p>"], "2025B-p3#x": ["<p>Take an arbitrary feasible point, not just another vertex. Express its objective using the basis dual vector and reduced costs.</p>", "<p>For standard-form minimization, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. Every feasible point has cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Basic reduced costs are zero; nonnegative nonbasic costs prove optimality.</p>", "<p>The equality constraints make the dual-vector term constant across feasible points. The remaining nonbasic contribution is nonnegative, while it vanishes at the given basic solution.</p>", "<p>Fix the sign convention, derive the cost difference from an arbitrary feasible point, use nonnegativity for optimality, and inspect equality separately for uniqueness.</p>", "<p>Show the cost comparison for every feasible point, and verify the given basic solution is feasible. Nonnegative reduced costs do not generally prove uniqueness.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p1#x": ["<p>Choose variables for amounts of each food item and attach units. Translate each nutritional lower and upper requirement separately.</p>", "<p>LP variables are continuous and constraints and objectives are linear. For an absolute value of affine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, minimize an auxiliary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. State variable meanings and units.</p>", "<p>The objective sums price times amount. Encode total-weight and nutritional bounds with the correct inequality directions, then solve the resulting LP if requested.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, translate every condition, and write the objective. Justify both directions. If solving numerically, report a feasible solution, its value and an optimality check.</p>", "<p>Report actual amounts and total cost, verify all nutritional constraints numerically, and do not impose integrality unless portions are required to be discrete.</p>"], "hw2026s4-p1#b": ["<p>Start from any feasible point of the original system and consider an arbitrary perturbation with the specified small radius.</p>", "<p>For a row vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> and Euclidean perturbation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>, Cauchy–Schwarz gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Thus a norm bound on every row controls how much every inequality can change inside a ball.</p>", "<p>For each row, bound the change in its left side by the row norm times the perturbation norm. Use the bit-length bound on coefficients to compare this with the added slack.</p>", "<p>Choose an original feasible centre, bound each row's change for every allowed perturbation, compare it with the relaxation slack using the coefficient-size bounds, and conclude ball containment.</p>", "<p>Control every row and match the norm used for the ball. This argument proves an inner ball for the relaxed system, not for the original boundary point.</p>"], "hw2025s3-p4#x": ["<p>Use one nonnegative shipment variable for each permitted source–destination pair.</p>", "<p>LP variables are continuous and constraints and objectives are linear. For an absolute value of affine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, minimize an auxiliary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. State variable meanings and units.</p>", "<p>Translate supply restrictions and demand requirements with their correct directions, and sum per-unit cost times shipments. If total supply and demand differ, do not automatically turn every row into an equality.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, translate every condition, and write the objective. Justify both directions. If solving numerically, report a feasible solution, its value and an optimality check.</p>", "<p>Verify feasibility and the objective of the computed solution. Explain the chosen handling of unused supply or unmet demand according to the problem statement.</p>"], "hw2025s4-p2#a": ["<p>Write the entering and leaving indices at the specified pivot and the exact inequality required for a row to participate in the ratio test.</p>", "<p>A simplex cycle uses degenerate pivots without objective improvement. Bland's rule uses the smallest eligible index for entering and tied leaving choices. The proof compares reduced costs across bases with a nullspace pivot direction.</p>", "<p>Use Bland's smallest-index choice and the definition of the largest cycling index to derive the requested index comparison and reduced-cost signs.</p>", "<p>Use the exact named bases and index ordering. Prove coordinate signs separately, track which coordinates vanish, then combine them, retaining the strict inequality needed for contradiction.</p>", "<p>Distinguish strict eligibility from a zero ratio. Quote the rule that yields each sign instead of treating all pivot-column entries alike.</p>"], "hw2025s4-p2#b": ["<p>Construct the pivot direction by setting the entering coordinate to one and other nonbasic coordinates to zero.</p>", "<p>A simplex cycle uses degenerate pivots without objective improvement. Bland's rule uses the smallest eligible index for entering and tied leaving choices. The proof compares reduced costs across bases with a nullspace pivot direction.</p>", "<p>Solve for its basic coordinates from the basis equations so the equality-constraint product is zero. Then use part (a)'s pivot-column signs for the particular coordinate inequalities requested.</p>", "<p>Use the exact named bases and index ordering. Prove coordinate signs separately, track which coordinates vanish, then combine them, retaining the strict inequality needed for contradiction.</p>", "<p>Do not claim every component of the direction is nonnegative; only the coordinates specified by the argument need that sign. Verify the nullspace equation.</p>"], "hw2025s4-p2#c": ["<p>Move to the new named basis and distinguish its basic and nonbasic indices before discussing reduced costs.</p>", "<p>A simplex cycle uses degenerate pivots without objective improvement. Bland's rule uses the smallest eligible index for entering and tied leaving choices. The proof compares reduced costs across bases with a nullspace pivot direction.</p>", "<p>Basic reduced costs vanish. For earlier eligible nonbasic indices, use the smallest-index entering rule to constrain their signs at this later dictionary.</p>", "<p>Use the exact named bases and index ordering. Prove coordinate signs separately, track which coordinates vanish, then combine them, retaining the strict inequality needed for contradiction.</p>", "<p>Keep the old and new reduced-cost vectors distinct. Every sign should be justified using the basis where it is measured.</p>"], "hw2025s4-p2#d": ["<p>Write each reduced-cost vector as the original cost vector minus a row-space vector.</p>", "<p>A simplex cycle uses degenerate pivots without objective improvement. Bland's rule uses the smallest eligible index for entering and tied leaving choices. The proof compares reduced costs across bases with a nullspace pivot direction.</p>", "<p>Subtract the two expressions. The original costs cancel, so their difference lies in the row space of the constraint matrix and is orthogonal to the nullspace direction from part (b).</p>", "<p>Use the exact named bases and index ordering. Prove coordinate signs separately, track which coordinates vanish, then combine them, retaining the strict inequality needed for contradiction.</p>", "<p>Check subtraction signs and prove the dot product is zero algebraically. Do not assume the direction is feasible at both bases.</p>"], "hw2025s4-p2#e": ["<p>For each relevant index range, write the difference of the old and new reduced costs defining the requested vector.</p>", "<p>A simplex cycle uses degenerate pivots without objective improvement. Bland's rule uses the smallest eligible index for entering and tied leaving choices. The proof compares reduced costs across bases with a nullspace pivot direction.</p>", "<p>Apply the sign facts from parts (a) and (c), splitting out the special entering/leaving indices and coordinates that vanish.</p>", "<p>Use the exact named bases and index ordering. Prove coordinate signs separately, track which coordinates vanish, then combine them, retaining the strict inequality needed for contradiction.</p>", "<p>Keep weak and strict inequalities separate. The final contradiction needs at least one strictly positive contribution, not just nonnegativity everywhere.</p>"], "hw2025s4-p2#f": ["<p>Expand the dot product coordinate by coordinate and reuse the sign information established in the preceding parts.</p>", "<p>A simplex cycle uses degenerate pivots without objective improvement. Bland's rule uses the smallest eligible index for entering and tied leaving choices. The proof compares reduced costs across bases with a nullspace pivot direction.</p>", "<p>Show each nonzero contribution is nonnegative and identify the distinguished index giving a strict positive term. Compare this with part (d)'s zero dot product.</p>", "<p>Use the exact named bases and index ordering. Prove coordinate signs separately, track which coordinates vanish, then combine them, retaining the strict inequality needed for contradiction.</p>", "<p>State exactly which cycling assumption has been contradicted. Do not conclude from a weak nonnegative bound alone.</p>"], "2017A-p6#d": ["<p>Mark each original variable as nonnegative, nonpositive or free before rewriting any rows.</p>", "<p>Standard form here is equality constraints and nonnegative variables. Add nonnegative slack to a ‘less than’ row, subtract it from a ‘greater than’ row, and split a free variable into the difference of two nonnegative ones.</p>", "<p>Substitute for exceptional-sign variables, introduce slacks with the correct signs, and retain existing equalities. Apply any objective-direction conversion explicitly.</p>", "<p>Handle variable signs first, convert each row, then present objective, equalities and nonnegativity together. Explain how the transformed solution represents the original one.</p>", "<p>Check the transformed variable domains and give the map back to the original variables. A slack is not free.</p>"], "2014A-p10#a": ["<p>For every inequality, identify which side needs a nonnegative slack to make equality.</p>", "<p>Standard form here is equality constraints and nonnegative variables. Add nonnegative slack to a ‘less than’ row, subtract it from a ‘greater than’ row, and split a free variable into the difference of two nonnegative ones.</p>", "<p>Write all equations together with original and slack-variable nonnegativity, and give slack variables zero objective coefficients.</p>", "<p>Handle variable signs first, convert each row, then present objective, equalities and nonnegativity together. Explain how the transformed solution represents the original one.</p>", "<p>Verify that projecting a standard-form feasible point gives an original feasible point and that every original feasible point has the corresponding nonnegative slacks.</p>"], "2014A-p10#b": ["<p>Use the specified basis, not the usual all-slack basis. Set every variable outside that basis to zero.</p>", "<p>For basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, set nonbasic coordinates to zero and solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Express basics and the objective in nonbasic variables. The entering rule chooses improvement; the eligible minimum-ratio test preserves nonnegativity.</p>", "<p>Solve the remaining equality system for the two named basic variables. Then read the original-variable coordinates of the associated point.</p>", "<p>Write the dictionary, show the entering choice and eligible ratios, identify the leaving variable, update the point, verify feasibility, and recompute the objective.</p>", "<p>Check all solved coordinates are nonnegative and satisfy the original equations. A basis solution is not automatically feasible.</p>"], "2014A-p10#c": ["<p>Rewrite the objective as a function of nonbasic variables at the current basis.</p>", "<p>For basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, set nonbasic coordinates to zero and solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Express basics and the objective in nonbasic variables. The entering rule chooses improvement; the eligible minimum-ratio test preserves nonnegativity.</p>", "<p>Identify which nonbasic coefficients permit improvement under the objective direction, then apply the stated pivot selection rule to choose among them.</p>", "<p>Write the dictionary, show the entering choice and eligible ratios, identify the leaving variable, update the point, verify feasibility, and recompute the objective.</p>", "<p>Do not use raw objective coefficients before eliminating basic variables. State any tie-breaking rule used.</p>"], "2014A-p10#d": ["<p>Increase the entering variable and write how each basic variable changes.</p>", "<p>For basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, set nonbasic coordinates to zero and solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Express basics and the objective in nonbasic variables. The entering rule chooses improvement; the eligible minimum-ratio test preserves nonnegativity.</p>", "<p>Only basics that decrease can block the movement. Compute their allowed step sizes, take the smallest, and use the tie rule if necessary.</p>", "<p>Write the dictionary, show the entering choice and eligible ratios, identify the leaving variable, update the point, verify feasibility, and recompute the objective.</p>", "<p>Do not include rows with the wrong pivot sign or zero denominator. Confirm that all basic variables remain nonnegative at the chosen step.</p>"], "2014A-p10#e": ["<p>Use the actual step size and new point from the previous pivot parts.</p>", "<p>For basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, set nonbasic coordinates to zero and solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Express basics and the objective in nonbasic variables. The entering rule chooses improvement; the eligible minimum-ratio test preserves nonnegativity.</p>", "<p>Evaluate the original objective at that point, or add the reduced-cost change times the step to the old objective as a cross-check.</p>", "<p>Write the dictionary, show the entering choice and eligible ratios, identify the leaving variable, update the point, verify feasibility, and recompute the objective.</p>", "<p>Report the objective value, not only the entering coordinate. A degenerate pivot can have zero objective change.</p>"], "2015A-p7#a": ["<p>Add slacks, identify the starting basis and write each basic variable in terms of nonbasic ones.</p>", "<p>For basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, set nonbasic coordinates to zero and solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Express basics and the objective in nonbasic variables. The entering rule chooses improvement; the eligible minimum-ratio test preserves nonnegativity.</p>", "<p>Use the objective dictionary to choose an entering variable, the ratio test to select a leaving variable, and solve the pivot row before substituting into the other rows.</p>", "<p>Write the dictionary, show the entering choice and eligible ratios, identify the leaving variable, update the point, verify feasibility, and recompute the objective.</p>", "<p>Present the new dictionary and check the new basic point. Track the constant objective term as well as coefficients.</p>"], "2015A-p7#d": ["<p>Give each of the two absolute-value terms its own auxiliary variable.</p>", "<p>LP variables are continuous and constraints and objectives are linear. For an absolute value of affine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, minimize an auxiliary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. State variable meanings and units.</p>", "<p>For each auxiliary variable, impose two inequalities bounding the corresponding affine expression and its negation. Minimize the sum of the auxiliary variables.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, translate every condition, and write the objective. Justify both directions. If solving numerically, report a feasible solution, its value and an optimality check.</p>", "<p>Explain why minimization makes these bounds tight. Do not restrict the original free variables to be nonnegative without representing their negative values.</p>"], "2016A-p6#d": ["<p>List all original rows and variable-sign conditions. Decide each required substitution before adding slacks.</p>", "<p>Standard form here is equality constraints and nonnegative variables. Add nonnegative slack to a ‘less than’ row, subtract it from a ‘greater than’ row, and split a free variable into the difference of two nonnegative ones.</p>", "<p>Turn every inequality into equality using the appropriate sign of a nonnegative slack, and rewrite the objective under any variable substitutions.</p>", "<p>Handle variable signs first, convert each row, then present objective, equalities and nonnegativity together. Explain how the transformed solution represents the original one.</p>", "<p>Check that the final form has only nonnegative variables and equalities, with no omitted original sign condition.</p>"], "2016A-p6#e": ["<p>Set the nonbasic variables to zero according to the particular basis given in the question.</p>", "<p>For basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, set nonbasic coordinates to zero and solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Express basics and the objective in nonbasic variables. The entering rule chooses improvement; the eligible minimum-ratio test preserves nonnegativity.</p>", "<p>Solve the two remaining basic variables from the standard-form equations and project to the original variables.</p>", "<p>Write the dictionary, show the entering choice and eligible ratios, identify the leaving variable, update the point, verify feasibility, and recompute the objective.</p>", "<p>Check nonnegativity and feasibility. Do not assume the basis point is optimal merely because it is feasible.</p>"], "2018A-p3#d": ["<p>Introduce an auxiliary variable for the absolute-value objective, keeping the original feasibility inequality.</p>", "<p>LP variables are continuous and constraints and objectives are linear. For an absolute value of affine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, minimize an auxiliary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. State variable meanings and units.</p>", "<p>Bound the auxiliary variable below by both the affine expression inside the absolute value and its negation; minimize it.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, translate every condition, and write the objective. Justify both directions. If solving numerically, report a feasible solution, its value and an optimality check.</p>", "<p>The original constraint remains in the LP. Justify equality with the absolute value at an optimum, not at every feasible point.</p>"], "2023B-p1#a": ["<p>Ask whether the assumptions ensure the primal has a finite attained optimum.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>A nonempty bounded feasible polyhedron and a linear objective give a finite optimum. Invoke the precise strong-duality theorem to conclude existence and equality of the dual optimum.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Mention feasibility and finiteness before applying strong duality. Boundedness of the feasible region is sufficient here, not always necessary.</p>"], "2024A-p1#a": ["<p>Write weak duality with the correct primal objective direction. If both problems have feasible points, can either improve without bound?</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>A feasible solution on each side bounds the other's objective. This yields finite optima, after which strong duality forces their equality.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Compare optimal values, not arbitrary feasible values. Feasible primal and dual points can have different objective values even though their optima coincide.</p>"], "2023A-p4#b": ["<p>Assign one dual variable to each vertex-capacity row in the fractional matching LP.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Each edge variable touches its two endpoint rows, so its dual constraint combines those two endpoint prices. Use the edge's objective coefficient on the other side.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Check max/min direction, nonnegativity and edge-indexed constraints. Remove slack-variable artifacts only with their implied sign conditions preserved.</p>"], "2023A-p4#c": ["<p>Interpret choosing an integer dual variable at a vertex. What does each edge constraint require of its endpoints?</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>For nonnegative unit-cost vertex choices, values above one can be reduced to one without destroying edge coverage. Identify the resulting binary covering problem.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>State the combinatorial problem and justify objective and feasibility. Do not infer equality between integer matching and integer cover optima for arbitrary nonbipartite graphs.</p>"], "2023B-p2#x": ["<p>Take arbitrary feasible primal and dual vectors under the question's standard-form convention.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Multiply the dual inequalities by nonnegative primal coordinates, then use the primal equalities to replace the matrix–vector product. This creates the objective inequality directly.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Write every inequality direction and the nonnegativity that justifies it. Do not invoke strong duality to prove weak duality.</p>"], "2024C-p6#x": ["<p>Apply the permitted Farkas form to primal infeasibility and keep the existing feasible dual point as a starting point.</p>", "<p>A Farkas alternative says exactly one holds: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is feasible; or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. A certificate can yield a feasible improving ray. Do not use strong duality circularly when proving it.</p>", "<p>Translate the certificate into a direction preserving all dual inequalities. Add nonnegative multiples of it to the feasible dual point and show the objective improves without bound.</p>", "<p>Transform to the permitted Farkas form, derive the certificate with correct signs, construct the required direction, and prove feasibility and objective improvement along the entire ray.</p>", "<p>State feasibility for every ray parameter and a strict objective-improvement sign. Do not assume an optimum exists in the infeasible case.</p>"], "2025A-p2#a": ["<p>Write one dual variable for each row of the inequality system and apply the minimization convention carefully.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Transpose the constraint matrix in the dual, then use its symmetry to simplify the expression. Keep objective coefficients and right-hand-side coefficients in their proper roles.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Symmetry does not remove the reversal of inequality direction or max/min objective. Check all variable signs.</p>"], "2025A-p2#b": ["<p>Try using the proposed vector as both a primal candidate and a dual candidate from part (a).</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Use the equality involving the symmetric matrix to verify both feasibility systems. Evaluate both objectives at this vector and compare them using weak duality.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Show both feasibility checks and equal objective values. Merely satisfying a primal constraint at equality does not by itself certify optimality.</p>"], "2025C-p2#a": ["<p>Give the set-cover LP one row for each element and one variable for each available set.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>A dual variable prices an element; each set imposes an upper bound on the sum of prices of elements it contains. Derive this through the requested standard-form conversion.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Match weights, incidence indices and signs with the displayed dual. Do not skip the conversion if the question explicitly asks for it.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p2#x": ["<p>Start from your actual diet LP in the previous problem, including every lower bound, upper bound and equality.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Convert to one chosen convention, assign dual variables to all rows, and interpret them as prices for the corresponding resource requirements. Solve and compare primal and dual objectives.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Give feasible solutions on both sides and equal values within the stated numerical tolerance. Opposite-direction nutritional constraints require different dual signs.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p4#x": ["<p>Encode the equality as two inequalities and nonnegativity as additional inequalities so the class version applies.</p>", "<p>A Farkas alternative says exactly one holds: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is feasible; or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. A certificate can yield a feasible improving ray. Do not use strong duality circularly when proving it.</p>", "<p>Split the resulting nonnegative multipliers by their row groups. Subtract the two equality-row multiplier vectors to obtain a free certificate vector with the needed matrix and objective signs.</p>", "<p>Transform to the permitted Farkas form, derive the certificate with correct signs, construct the required direction, and prove feasibility and objective improvement along the entire ray.</p>", "<p>Prove mutual exclusion as well as existence of a certificate when infeasible. Check signs after recombining the multipliers.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p5#a": ["<p>Use the Farkas variant just proved for the infeasible primal standard-form system.</p>", "<p>A Farkas alternative says exactly one holds: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is feasible; or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. A certificate can yield a feasible improving ray. Do not use strong duality circularly when proving it.</p>", "<p>Combine its certificate with any feasible dual point to construct an improving ray. The certificate's strict inner-product sign supplies unbounded improvement.</p>", "<p>Transform to the permitted Farkas form, derive the certificate with correct signs, construct the required direction, and prove feasibility and objective improvement along the entire ray.</p>", "<p>Check every dual constraint along the ray, not only its objective. Cite the exact certificate inequalities from the preceding question.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p5#b": ["<p>Put the infeasible dual inequalities in the form of the class Farkas lemma.</p>", "<p>A Farkas alternative says exactly one holds: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is feasible; or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. A certificate can yield a feasible improving ray. Do not use strong duality circularly when proving it.</p>", "<p>Its multipliers provide a primal-feasible direction: the homogeneous constraints are preserved while the objective improves. Add this direction to the supplied feasible primal point.</p>", "<p>Transform to the permitted Farkas form, derive the certificate with correct signs, construct the required direction, and prove feasibility and objective improvement along the entire ray.</p>", "<p>Confirm the direction preserves nonnegativity for all nonnegative multiples and improves the objective in the primal's correct direction.</p>"], "hw2026s4-p1#a": ["<p>Choose the infeasibility certificate whose size or normalized margin can be controlled by the stated vertex bound.</p>", "<p>A Farkas alternative says exactly one holds: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is feasible; or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. A certificate can yield a feasible improving ray. Do not use strong duality circularly when proving it.</p>", "<p>For the relaxed right-hand side, the certificate value increases by the relaxation amount times the sum of certificate multipliers. Bound this increase strictly below the original negative margin.</p>", "<p>Transform to the permitted Farkas form, derive the certificate with correct signs, construct the required direction, and prove feasibility and objective improvement along the entire ray.</p>", "<p>An arbitrary unnormalized certificate does not justify the numerical tolerance. Track the bit-length bounds and the strict inequality needed to preserve infeasibility.</p>"], "hw2025s4-p1#x": ["<p>Write the target infeasible system in the class lemma's inequality format, including sign constraints as rows.</p>", "<p>A Farkas alternative says exactly one holds: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is feasible; or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. A certificate can yield a feasible improving ray. Do not use strong duality circularly when proving it.</p>", "<p>Partition the certificate multipliers into their row groups and eliminate auxiliary components to derive exactly the desired nonnegative vector and matrix inequality.</p>", "<p>Transform to the permitted Farkas form, derive the certificate with correct signs, construct the required direction, and prove feasibility and objective improvement along the entire ray.</p>", "<p>Check the target lemma's exact primal form and both alternatives. A certificate must satisfy the strict negative scalar inequality as well as all weak inequalities.</p>"], "hw2025s4-p3#a": ["<p>Take the primal infeasibility certificate and separately name a feasible dual starting point.</p>", "<p>A Farkas alternative says exactly one holds: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is feasible; or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. A certificate can yield a feasible improving ray. Do not use strong duality circularly when proving it.</p>", "<p>Build a ray by adding the certificate direction to that point. Verify the dual constraints and show its objective is unbounded in the improving direction.</p>", "<p>Transform to the permitted Farkas form, derive the certificate with correct signs, construct the required direction, and prove feasibility and objective improvement along the entire ray.</p>", "<p>Explain why dual feasibility is needed: a direction by itself is not a feasible ray unless it has a feasible starting point.</p>"], "hw2025s4-p3#b": ["<p>Apply Farkas to the dual infeasibility statement, with its variable signs included.</p>", "<p>A Farkas alternative says exactly one holds: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is feasible; or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. A certificate can yield a feasible improving ray. Do not use strong duality circularly when proving it.</p>", "<p>Turn the resulting multipliers into a homogeneous primal direction and add it to the feasible primal point. Use the strict certificate sign to show unbounded objective improvement.</p>", "<p>Transform to the permitted Farkas form, derive the certificate with correct signs, construct the required direction, and prove feasibility and objective improvement along the entire ray.</p>", "<p>Check all direction constraints and signs. Do not simply cite the other part without explaining how the roles and conventions change.</p>"], "2017A-p6#b": ["<p>For the hitting-set relaxation, assign a dual variable to each subset that must be hit.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Each selectable element limits the total dual weight of subsets containing it. Write the dual objective using the original coverage right-hand sides.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Do not exchange elements and subsets midway. State all dual variable domains and the correct objective direction.</p>"], "2017A-p6#c": ["<p>Read the dual's integer variables as choices of subsets. What does the constraint for one element prevent?</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>With unit capacities, no two selected subsets may share an element. Translate the objective into the number or weight of selected pairwise disjoint subsets.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Explain feasibility and objective in both directions, accounting for any exceptional empty subsets permitted by the instance.</p>"], "2014B-p8#c": ["<p>Place a dual variable on each edge constraint of the vertex-cover relaxation.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Each vertex gives one upper bound on the sum of incident edge variables. Derive the dual objective from the unit coverage right-hand sides.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Check that edges index variables and vertices index constraints; do not reverse them. Preserve nonnegativity.</p>"], "2014B-p8#d": ["<p>Make the edge variables of the preceding dual integral. Interpret the incident-edge capacity at a vertex.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>With unit vertex capacities and nonnegative integer edge variables, feasible selected edges cannot share endpoints. The objective counts selected edges.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Identify the integer problem, not a claimed general integrality theorem. Fractional and integer optima need not agree outside special graph classes.</p>"], "2015A-p7#b": ["<p>Use the original maximization LP, not a partially pivoted dictionary, to identify rows, variables and signs.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Give each primal row a dual variable, transpose coefficients, and use right-hand sides in the dual objective and primal costs in dual constraints.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Check equalities and free variables separately. Write the complete dual with domains rather than just its matrix inequality.</p>"], "2015A-p7#c": ["<p>To lower-bound a minimization dual optimum, look for a feasible maximization primal point with the requested value.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Substitute a candidate into every primal constraint and calculate its objective. Weak duality then transfers that value into a lower bound for all dual feasible objectives.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>You do not need to solve either problem optimally to prove this lower bound. Feasibility of the witness is essential.</p>"], "2015B-p7#e": ["<p>Use one nonnegative dual variable per edge constraint of the independent-set relaxation.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>The dual constraint for a vertex collects weights of incident edges and requires enough total weight to cover its primal objective coefficient.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>If isolated vertices or explicit upper bounds are present, include their effect instead of silently discarding primal constraints.</p>"], "2015B-p7#f": ["<p>Interpret integer edge variables in the covering constraints of the preceding dual.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Each vertex must be incident to at least one selected edge, while the objective minimizes selected edges or their weights. Identify the edge-cover problem and its feasibility condition.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Isolated vertices make an ordinary edge cover impossible. Mention the graph assumption rather than calling this vertex cover.</p>"], "2016A-p6#b": ["<p>Put a dual variable at each vertex-capacity constraint in the fractional maximum-weight matching LP.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>For each edge, require the sum of its endpoint dual values to dominate its weight. Sum vertex-capacity contributions in the dual objective.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Edge weights belong on the right side of the edge constraints, not automatically as vertex objective weights. Check the exact primal capacities.</p>"], "2016A-p6#c": ["<p>Read the weighted matching dual as assigning nonnegative prices to vertices, constrained by each edge's weight.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Describe the covering requirement precisely: the sum of endpoint prices must cover the edge demand. Compare with the unweighted unit-demand vertex-cover special case.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Do not silently call this ordinary weighted vertex cover if weights appear as edge demands. State the variables, constraints and objective that the dual actually has.</p>"], "2017B-p2#d": ["<p>Retain both the knapsack capacity inequality and the item upper bounds in the relaxation.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Introduce one dual price for capacity and one for each item upper bound. The constraint for an item combines its size times capacity price with its own upper-bound price.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Omitting item upper bounds changes the primal and its dual. Include all dual terms in the objective.</p>"], "2018A-p3#c": ["<p>Perfect-matching degree constraints are equalities, unlike ordinary matching's at-most-one constraints.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Assign a free dual variable to each vertex equality. For minimization, each edge gives an upper bound on the sum of endpoint dual variables by its cost.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Explain why vertex dual variables are free. Confirm which redundant primal constraints were removed in the previous part.</p>"], "2021A-p2#2": ["<p>Write the specific course–student incidence matrix with rows and columns explicitly labelled.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Derive the continuous LP dual first, preserving any upper bounds on student variables. Only then impose the integrality required for the requested dual BIP.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>The phrase ‘dual BIP’ is a course modelling convention, not standard strong duality for integer programs. Check dimensions and all upper-bound contributions.</p>"], "2021A-p2#3": ["<p>Use the dual constraints to identify what must cover one student's contribution and which course decisions provide it.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Interpret each dual variable and coefficient from the preceding matrix formulation, including any auxiliary variables introduced for bounds. Describe the objective as the resulting covering cost.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Do not infer the interpretation from the problem name alone. Explain every constraint family and why binary choices represent the intended cover.</p>"], "2021B-p1#2": ["<p>Form the bipartite incidence matrix and record the possibly different capacities on the two sides.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>Derive the LP dual with one price per vertex row. The capacity vector becomes objective weights; each edge imposes an endpoint-price covering constraint.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Use the actual capacities from this instance and preserve variable domains. Impose integrality only after the LP dual is derived.</p>"], "2021B-p1#3": ["<p>Read integral vertex prices as choices covering each edge of the bipartite graph.</p>", "<p>The minimization problem with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has a maximization dual with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Equalities give free dual variables; free primal variables give dual equalities. Feasible equal-value pairs certify optimality.</p>", "<p>For unit edge demands, reduce chosen integral prices to binary values where valid. Vertex capacities from the primal determine the cover costs in the dual objective.</p>", "<p>Choose a convention. Give one dual variable per primal row and one dual constraint per primal variable. Check signs and dimensions, then interpret variables, constraints and objective if requested.</p>", "<p>Explain why this is weighted vertex cover and where those weights come from. Prove the interpretation in both directions.</p>"], "2023A-p2#x": ["<p>Use one binary variable per vertex to mean it is chosen in the dominating set.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>For each vertex, require at least one selected vertex in its closed neighbourhood: include the vertex itself as well as its neighbours. Minimize the number selected.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Check isolated vertices and self-domination. Using only open neighbourhoods models total domination, a different problem.</p>"], "2024A-p4#x": ["<p>Use binary truth-assignment variables and a separate variable recording whether each clause is counted as satisfied.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Translate a positive literal as its variable and a negative literal as one minus its variable. Bound a clause's counted indicator by the sum of its true-literal expressions, and maximize weighted counted clauses.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Explain why nonnegative clause weights make the objective set every satisfiable indicator appropriately. Keep all expressions linear.</p>"], "2024B-p1#a": ["<p>Pick an NP-hard selection problem that has a polynomial-size binary linear formulation.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>For independent set, use one binary vertex variable and at-most-one constraints for adjacent pairs. Show a polynomial-time integer-program optimizer would solve its decision version.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>State polynomial construction size and the decision threshold. Do not rely on LP solvability to claim integer programs are easy.</p>"], "2025A-p3#x": ["<p>Use a binary variable for assigning each job to each machine and a separate makespan bound.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Require each job to be assigned exactly once. For every machine, bound the sum of its assigned processing times by the makespan variable, then minimize that variable.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Include assignment domains and all machine-load constraints. Do not sum machine loads in the objective when the goal is maximum load.</p>"], "2025B-p2#a": ["<p>Give each available set one binary selection variable.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>For each universe element, require the sum of selected sets containing it to be at least one. Minimize the weighted sum of selected sets.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Check element-indexed constraints, set-indexed variables and costs. Overlap between selected sets is allowed.</p>"], "2023B-p4#a": ["<p>Take any Hamiltonian cycle and set exactly its edge variables to one.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Check every degree constraint and domain using the fact that the cycle visits each vertex once. Compute the encoded objective from its selected edges.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>This part proves only one direction. Degree constraints alone have not yet established connectivity.</p>"], "2023B-p4#b": ["<p>Look for a collection of cycles that gives every vertex the required degree without forming one connected tour.</p>", "<p>One instance meeting all hypotheses but violating the conclusion disproves a universal claim. For LP-rounding claims, check both feasibility and any optimality requirement before rounding.</p>", "<p>Use two vertex-disjoint cycles on a suitable complete graph, set their edges to one, and check every degree constraint.</p>", "<p>Specify the instance, verify hypotheses, calculate the relevant output, and exhibit the violated constraint or ratio. Address ties if they affect the construction.</p>", "<p>Verify the chosen instance is large enough for both cycles under the graph convention. Explain precisely why it is not Hamiltonian.</p>"], "2023B-p4#c": ["<p>Start with a feasible integral degree-constrained solution, so its selected edges form disjoint cycles in the undirected setting.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>If more than one component exists, choose the vertex set of one component and apply its subtour-elimination constraint. The selected internal or crossing edge count contradicts that constraint.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Use the exact displayed subtour inequality and cover all proper nonempty subsets required. Conclude connectivity plus degree conditions gives a single cycle.</p>"], "2024C-p4#x": ["<p>Use binary item-to-bin assignments and binary bin-used flags. A number of candidate bins equal to the number of items is sufficient when each item fits individually.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Require each item in exactly one bin and constrain each bin's total size by capacity times its used flag. Add explicit assignment-to-used links if zero-size items could otherwise occupy an unused bin.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Minimize used bins, not assignments. Check oversized and zero-size items under the input assumptions and prove both modelling directions.</p>"], "hw2026s4-p3#a": ["<p>Let each subset have a binary variable recording whether it is selected.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>For every element, require at least one selected containing subset, and minimize the sum of the selection variables in the unweighted problem.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>State variable domains and explain that one selected subset may cover many elements. Do not require exact-one coverage.</p>"], "hw2026s4-p2#a": ["<p>A clique may contain any adjacent pair but must exclude choosing both endpoints of a nonedge.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Use one binary vertex variable, impose at-most-one constraints for every nonadjacent distinct pair, and maximize the sum of selected vertices.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Constraints go on nonedges, unlike independent set. Verify both a clique gives a feasible point and a feasible selected set is a clique.</p>"], "hw2026s4-p2#b": ["<p>Use one binary decision for whether each input integer is included.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Set the weighted sum of those decisions equal to the target. This is a feasibility problem; an arbitrary constant objective is enough if a solver requires one.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Use equality, not an upper bound. Preserve signs if the input integers can be negative and count each input position separately.</p>"], "hw2026s4-p2#c": ["<p>Use at most one candidate colour per vertex in the colour palette, with assignment variables and colour-used flags.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Require one colour per vertex, forbid adjacent vertices from sharing a colour, and link each assignment to the corresponding used flag. Minimize the number of used flags.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Keep products of binary variables out of the model. Include every assignment link; otherwise the objective can set used flags to zero.</p>"], "2014B-p8#a": ["<p>Interpret one binary vertex variable and ask what the inequality for an edge forces.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>If each edge requires at least one endpoint chosen and the objective counts chosen vertices, name the covering problem and match its feasible solutions.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Distinguish vertex cover from independent set: the direction of the edge inequality is decisive.</p>"], "2014B-p8#b": ["<p>Keep the vertex-cover objective and edge constraints unchanged.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Replace each binary domain with a continuous unit interval. If dropping upper bounds, explain why an optimum can be clipped at one without hurting feasibility or cost.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>A relaxation enlarges the feasible set. Do not round variables or reverse edge inequalities during this step.</p>"], "2017A-p6#a": ["<p>Use a binary variable for each ground-set element that may be selected into the hitting set.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>For every subset that must be hit, require the sum of its selected elements to be at least one. Minimize the number or stated weights of chosen elements.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Do not use one variable per subset unless modelling the dual covering problem. Handle empty required subsets as infeasibility.</p>"], "2017B-p2#b": ["<p>Use a binary variable for taking each item. Keep value and weight coefficients in separate roles.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Maximize total selected value subject to a single total-weight capacity inequality and binary domains.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Items are indivisible in the BIP. Verify that the objective uses values and the capacity constraint uses weights.</p>"], "2017B-p2#c": ["<p>Start with the BIP from the preceding part and change only the integrality restriction.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Allow each item-selection variable continuously between zero and one, retaining objective and capacity unchanged.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Nonnegativity alone would permit taking several copies of an item. Keep the upper bound unless it is individually implied.</p>"], "2018A-p3#a": ["<p>Use one binary edge variable and express the condition that every vertex is matched exactly once.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Require the sum of incident selected edges to equal one at each vertex and minimize the sum of edge costs times selections.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>An inequality at most one models nonperfect matching. Check feasibility in both directions and include all vertices.</p>"], "2018A-p3#b": ["<p>Remove integrality from the perfect-matching formulation while preserving degree equalities.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Keep edge variables nonnegative. Each incident degree equality already limits an edge variable to at most one, so identify which explicit bounds are redundant.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>This is the stated relaxation, not necessarily the full matching polytope for a general graph. Do not add or assume integrality without justification.</p>"], "2021A-p2#1": ["<p>Let a student's binary variable say whether that student is selected. Label the course membership of each student.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>For each course, bound the sum of selected enrolled students by its capacity. Maximize the total selected students and retain all binary domains.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>A student enrolled in several courses contributes to every relevant capacity row. Explain those overlapping constraints.</p>"], "2021B-p1#1": ["<p>Use one binary variable for each edge of the bipartite graph. Read the capacity allowed at each side in the semi-matching definition.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Write one incident-edge capacity constraint per vertex, using the appropriate side's capacity, and maximize the number of selected edges.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Do not replace the semi-matching capacities by ordinary matching capacities unless they are actually one. Verify every constraint family against the definition.</p>"], "2014A-p4#a": ["<p>Choose a binary variable per vertex and use the given vertex weights in the objective.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Each vertex must have a selected member of its closed neighbourhood. Minimize the total selected weight under these coverage constraints.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Include the vertex itself in its neighbourhood constraint and explain isolated vertices. Distinguish weights in the objective from adjacency coefficients.</p>"], "2014A-p4#b": ["<p>Try a small symmetric graph where every closed neighbourhood has the same size but no single vertex dominates the whole graph.</p>", "<p>One instance meeting all hypotheses but violating the conclusion disproves a universal claim. For LP-rounding claims, check both feasibility and any optimality requirement before rounding.</p>", "<p>For example, inspect a four-cycle with equal vertex weights. Assign each vertex the reciprocal of its closed-neighbourhood size and compare fractional cost with the smallest integral dominating set.</p>", "<p>Specify the instance, verify hypotheses, calculate the relevant output, and exhibit the violated constraint or ratio. Address ties if they affect the construction.</p>", "<p>Check every fractional coverage inequality and prove the integral lower bound. A fractional feasible point alone does not show the optimum is fractional unless its value beats all integral solutions.</p>"], "2014B-p7#a": ["<p>Use item-assignment variables and bin-used indicators, with at most one candidate bin per item.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Enforce one assignment per item and per-bin load at most capacity times its used flag. Minimize the number of used bins; add assignment links if zero-size items are allowed.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Check binary domains, all load constraints and the interpretation of unused bins. Never model the number of bins merely by total volume.</p>"], "2015B-p7#a": ["<p>Read an edge inequality saying its endpoint variables sum to at most one.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Interpret selected vertices as those with variable one. Each edge excludes simultaneous selection of its endpoints, and the objective counts selected vertices.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Explain why the selected vertices contain no edge, and why every such vertex set yields a feasible integer point.</p>"], "2015B-p7#b": ["<p>Keep the edge-conflict inequalities and objective from the integer formulation.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Allow continuous variables in the unit interval. If the source omits upper bounds, check whether every vertex has an incident edge before treating them as implied.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Isolated vertices need explicit upper bounds to prevent unboundedness. State the graph assumption or retain the bounds.</p>"], "2015B-p7#c": ["<p>Try assigning the same value to every vertex, chosen so every edge constraint is tight.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>Set each variable to one half, verify domains and edge inequalities, and compute the objective as a feasible lower bound for this maximization LP.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>A feasible point proves a lower bound on a maximization optimum, not an upper bound. Do not claim this point is always optimal.</p>"], "2015B-p7#d": ["<p>To force at most one selected vertex, ensure every pair of distinct vertices conflicts.</p>", "<p>One instance meeting all hypotheses but violating the conclusion disproves a universal claim. For LP-rounding claims, check both feasibility and any optimality requirement before rounding.</p>", "<p>Use a complete graph on the required number of vertices and compute its largest independent set. Compare with the relaxation if the surrounding question asks for a gap.</p>", "<p>Specify the instance, verify hypotheses, calculate the relevant output, and exhibit the violated constraint or ratio. Address ties if they affect the construction.</p>", "<p>Prove both that one vertex is feasible and that two are impossible. Keep the requested vertex count exact.</p>"], "2015B-p7#g": ["<p>Begin with a maximum matching and inspect its unmatched vertices. Assume there are no isolated vertices if an edge cover is required.</p>", "<p>An approximation needs a feasible output and a comparison with optimum. Charge optimal objects or necessary cost to algorithm choices, with bounded charges per choice. The ratio direction differs for minimization and maximization.</p>", "<p>Add one incident edge for every unmatched vertex. For optimality, use the relation between a minimum edge cover and a maximum matching, proving the needed lower bound via components of an inclusion-minimal edge cover.</p>", "<p>Describe the algorithm, prove feasibility, identify the optimum bound, prove charging multiplicity, derive the ratio in the correct direction, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Use a maximum matching, not just maximal. Explain why unmatched vertices cannot be adjacent and why the constructed number of edges is minimum.</p>"], "2016A-p6#a": ["<p>Use one binary variable per edge and the given edge weights in a maximization objective.</p>", "<p>Binary variables encode selected/not selected. Constraints must enforce every feasibility rule, including links between different variable families. Relaxing binary variables gives continuous values in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, unless upper bounds are proved redundant.</p>", "<p>At every vertex, limit the sum of incident selected edge variables to at most one.</p>", "<p>Define variables and domains, objective and constraint families. Prove correspondence in both directions, checking empty choices, unused objects and linking constraints.</p>", "<p>Do not use equality unless a perfect matching is required. Explain both the conflict constraints and the objective's correspondence to matching weight.</p>"], "2023A-p1#c": ["<p>Start by writing two rows: input in the language, input outside it. Put the acceptance bounds for each class in those rows. Do not start with a majority vote: both original bounds are below one half.</p>", "<p>In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a polynomial-time randomized algorithm accepts no-instances with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> and yes-instances with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> independent runs with acceptance rate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, the empirical rate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. A positive gap lets you place a decision threshold between the promised rates.</p>", "<p>For the amplification direction, count accepting runs and compare their fraction with the midpoint of one eighth and one quarter. The distance from either promised rate to this midpoint is one sixteenth. Choose enough independent trials for the tail probability to be at most the target error.</p>", "<p>Write both acceptance guarantees. Prove each inclusion separately, or give a counterexample. For amplification, specify a threshold, analyse both input cases, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>Have you proved both inclusions, analysed both input cases, and kept polynomial runtime? For the easy inclusion, finitely many short input lengths may need to be hardcoded.</p>"], "2024C-p1#c": ["<p>Write the two acceptance conditions when both thresholds equal one half. Ask whether an algorithm that ignores the input could satisfy both.</p>", "<p>In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a polynomial-time randomized algorithm accepts no-instances with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> and yes-instances with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> independent runs with acceptance rate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, the empirical rate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. A positive gap lets you place a decision threshold between the promised rates.</p>", "<p>Test the algorithm ‘flip a fair coin and accept on heads’. Then ask whether a genuine positive-gap, polynomial-time randomized decider could decide an undecidable language: enumerate its finitely many random-bit strings.</p>", "<p>Write both acceptance guarantees. Prove each inclusion separately, or give a counterexample. For amplification, specify a threshold, analyse both input cases, and bound runtime.</p>", "<p>To disprove equality, explain both why the no-gap class contains every language and why the positive-gap class contains only decidable languages. Merely saying ‘there is no gap’ is not a complete proof.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p3#d": ["<p>Write one indicator for a vertex surviving the threshold. The same random threshold is used for all vertices; do not invent separate independent thresholds.</p>", "<p>An indicator is 1 when its event happens and 0 otherwise. Its expectation is the probability of that event. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, without an independence requirement.</p>", "<p>For a fixed vertex, its survival event is that the uniform threshold lies between zero and its fractional vertex weight. Check that this weight lies in the unit interval, then use the length of that interval.</p>", "<p>Define the random experiment and one indicator. Express the count as a sum, calculate one event's probability, sum expectations, and state the conclusion.</p>", "<p>Did you define the indicator, justify the weight range and use linearity without claiming independence? The conclusion concerns the expected number of vertices, not a density ratio.</p>"], "hw2026s3-p3#e": ["<p>Repeat part (d)'s setup for edges, using the edge weight rather than a product of endpoint weights. Read the definition of the edge threshold set carefully.</p>", "<p>An indicator is 1 when its event happens and 0 otherwise. Its expectation is the probability of that event. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, without an independence requirement.</p>", "<p>An edge survives exactly when the common threshold is at most its fractional edge weight. Use feasibility to put that weight in the unit interval, compute its survival probability, and sum over edges.</p>", "<p>Define the random experiment and one indicator. Express the count as a sum, calculate one event's probability, sum expectations, and state the conclusion.</p>", "<p>Does your event use the edge weight from the LP? Multiplying endpoint probabilities would incorrectly assume independent endpoint thresholds.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p2#a": ["<p>First check the random-graph convention. For a uniformly random simple loopless regular graph, a fixed vertex has only the other vertices as possible neighbours; diagonal pairs are not exchangeable with off-diagonal pairs.</p>", "<p>An indicator is 1 when its event happens and 0 otherwise. Its expectation is the probability of that event. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, without an independence requirement.</p>", "<p>Define one edge indicator per ordered pair. In the simple loopless model symmetry gives probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for distinct endpoints, so the expectation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The supplied solution instead uses all pairs with denominator <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; that needs a different model and does not follow from relabeling symmetry alone.</p>", "<p>Define the random experiment and one indicator. Express the count as a sum, calculate one event's probability, sum expectations, and state the conclusion.</p>", "<p>This source has a convention issue, not a missing trick. State the assumed graph model and do not claim diagonal and off-diagonal pairs have identical probabilities. The later mixing-lemma main term is not automatically an exact random-graph expectation.</p>"], "hw2025s1-p1#a": ["<p>Make two headings: turn expected time into a timeout; turn ‘don't know’ into retries. These are two separate algorithms.</p>", "<p>ZPP allows zero error and expected polynomial time. Markov gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Independent trials that answer with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> need <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> trials in expectation.</p>", "<p>In the first direction, stop after a constant multiple of the expected polynomial bound and return ‘don't know’ on timeout. In the other, rerun independently until a committed answer appears; its constant success probability bounds the expected number of attempts.</p>", "<p>For each direction, describe the transformed algorithm, prove it cannot answer incorrectly, bound its timeout or retries, and establish the required runtime guarantee.</p>", "<p>Did you prove committed answers are always correct? Distinguish worst-case polynomial time for the timeout algorithm from expected polynomial time for repeated trials.</p>"], "hw2025s1-p1#b": ["<p>Write RP's acceptance guarantees separately for yes- and no-inputs. Identify which one needs improvement to meet the BPP definition.</p>", "<p>RP never falsely accepts; coRP never falsely rejects. The other error probability is at most one half. With independent runs, the probability all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> attempts fail is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Try two independent runs and accept if either accepts. Calculate the probability both reject on a yes-input. For coRP, reverse the roles: reject if either run rejects.</p>", "<p>Describe the output-combination rule. Check yes- and no-inputs separately, preserve the zero-error side, compare with the target thresholds, and count runtime.</p>", "<p>Have you specified OR versus AND correctly, preserved the error-free side, and checked both BPP thresholds? Repetition without an output rule is incomplete.</p>"], "hw2025s1-p1#c": ["<p>Use part (a)'s bounded-time ‘don't know’ characterization. For one inclusion, decide how to replace ‘don't know’; for the reverse, identify which outputs of RP and coRP are certain to be correct.</p>", "<p>ZPP allows zero error and expected polynomial time. Markov gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Independent trials that answer with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> need <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> trials in expectation.</p>", "<p>Replacing ‘don't know’ by reject yields an RP candidate, and replacing it by accept yields a coRP candidate. In the reverse direction, trust an RP acceptance or a coRP rejection; otherwise abstain. Amplify their uncertain sides first if necessary.</p>", "<p>For each direction, describe the transformed algorithm, prove it cannot answer incorrectly, bound its timeout or retries, and establish the required runtime guarantee.</p>", "<p>Check each input case: the two trusted outputs cannot contradict one another. Bound abstention and runtime, and explicitly conclude both inclusions.</p>"], "2017A-p1#a": ["<p>Write the three recursive calls and combine the split and combine costs into one local-work term. A bounded-size base case is constant asymptotically.</p>", "<p>Separate recursive work from work in the current call. A recursion tree sums costs across levels. If level costs shrink by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, their sum is at most the root cost divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Compare the total child work with the parent work. Three children each have one quarter of the input size, so linear local work decreases by a factor of three quarters per level. Alternatively compare the master-theorem exponent with one.</p>", "<p>Write the recurrence and base case. Bound each level's work, sum it and account for leaves. Give a matching lower bound when a tight bound is requested.</p>", "<p>Have you included all recursive calls, summed level work, bounded leaves and used the root for a matching lower bound? Do not multiply every level by the root's cost.</p>"], "2017A-p1#b": ["<p>Write the first three local costs while halving both matrix dimensions. There is one recursive call, not one per row or column.</p>", "<p>Separate recursive work from work in the current call. A recursion tree sums costs across levels. If level costs shrink by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, their sum is at most the root cost divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Factor out the initial sum of dimensions. The remaining level factors form a geometric series with ratio one half. Include the terminal row's work using its reduced width.</p>", "<p>Write the recurrence and base case. Bound each level's work, sum it and account for leaves. Give a matching lower bound when a tight bound is requested.</p>", "<p>Does your bound include the base-case row and a root-level lower bound? The recursion depth alone does not determine the total cost.</p>"], "2017A-p1#c": ["<p>Identify the two scalar coefficients in the expression. Is the operation a sum, a product, or something nonlinear?</p>", "<p>For finite expectations, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, whether or not the variables are independent. Factoring an expectation of a product is a different operation.</p>", "<p>Apply linearity with coefficients two and minus one. If the dependence distracts you, expand expectation as a weighted sum over outcomes and distribute the sum.</p>", "<p>Identify the coefficients, state linearity, substitute, and explicitly address the dependence mentioned in the question.</p>", "<p>Mention that independence is unnecessary for finite expectations. Do not justify this using the product rule for independent variables.</p>"], "2018A-p1#b": ["<p>Write the recurrence from the two unequal child sizes and the dominant local work. The usual equal-size master theorem does not directly fit.</p>", "<p>Separate recursive work from work in the current call. A recursion tree sums costs across levels. If level costs shrink by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, their sum is at most the root cost divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Square each child-size fraction and add them to obtain the ratio of child-level quadratic work to parent work. This ratio is strictly less than one, so use a geometric upper bound.</p>", "<p>Write the recurrence and base case. Bound each level's work, sum it and account for leaves. Give a matching lower bound when a tight bound is requested.</p>", "<p>Did you square the fractions because local work is quadratic, account for leaves, and provide the root's matching lower bound?</p>"], "2018B-p4#a": ["<p>Imagine the input array is fixed adversarially before any pivot is chosen. Is there still randomness left in the execution?</p>", "<p>Fix the input and average over the algorithm's random choices. A worst-input expected guarantee is not a guarantee for every random seed. Selection recurses on one pivot side; quicksort processes both.</p>", "<p>Check whether the selection analysis assumes a distribution on input arrays or only a uniform pivot among the current elements. This determines whether its expected bound applies to every fixed input.</p>", "<p>State what is fixed and what is random. Explain how pivot choices determine runtime and why the argument does or does not depend on the input arrangement.</p>", "<p>Do not confuse worst-case input with worst-case random choices. State explicitly what the expectation averages over.</p>"], "2018B-p4#b": ["<p>Name the random experiment that produces two potentially different runtimes on the same fixed input.</p>", "<p>Fix the input and average over the algorithm's random choices. A worst-input expected guarantee is not a guarantee for every random seed. Selection recurses on one pivot side; quicksort processes both.</p>", "<p>Describe the sequence of random pivot choices made during the run. Their ranks determine subsequent subproblem sizes; the input is held fixed while taking the expectation.</p>", "<p>State what is fixed and what is random. Explain how pivot choices determine runtime and why the argument does or does not depend on the input arrangement.</p>", "<p>Do not average uniformly over all possible full pivot sequences: they need not have equal probabilities because their conditional subproblem sizes differ.</p>"], "2018B-p4#c": ["<p>For pivot rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, write the two side sizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Selection pays for at most one side after partitioning.</p>", "<p>Condition on a uniformly random pivot rank: each rank has probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Add linear partition work to the conditional expected cost of the one recursive call.</p>", "<p>Use a nondecreasing worst-case expected-cost bound for the larger possible side, then average over pivot ranks. For even input size, each upper-half side size occurs twice. Handle rounding as permitted by the question.</p>", "<p>Fix the requested order statistic, condition on pivot rank, bound the recursive side, average, and pair symmetric ranks. Follow the question's rounding convention.</p>", "<p>Explain the factor two and the summation limits. Do not sum both recursive sides as quicksort would, or silently assume a size-dependent runtime is monotone without using a monotone upper bound.</p>"], "2018B-p4#d": ["<p>Write the target as an explicit upper bound with an unknown constant, and replace the partition-cost term by a second constant times input size.</p>", "<p>For a linear bound, write nonrecursive cost as at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and try the strong-induction hypothesis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> for all smaller sizes. One constant must cover both the step and base cases.</p>", "<p>Substitute the linear inductive hypothesis into the upper-half sum. For even input size, the sum of its indices is at most three eighths of the square of the input size. The recursive contribution then uses at most three quarters of the desired linear bound.</p>", "<p>State the claim and base cases, substitute into the recurrence, bound the sum, solve for the necessary constant, and choose it large enough for every requirement.</p>", "<p>Choose the induction constant at least four times the partition-cost constant and large enough for base cases. Use inequalities rather than an approximate arithmetic-series calculation in the proof.</p>"], "2024B-p3#x": ["<p>Let the number of distinct minimum cuts be an unknown integer. Fix one of those cuts before running contraction; do not merely use the probability of finding some minimum cut.</p>", "<p>Karger's algorithm outputs any fixed minimum cut of a connected undirected multigraph with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Different output cuts are disjoint events. Keep parallel edges and discard self-loops during contraction.</p>", "<p>For each cut, define the event that it is the final output. Sum the per-cut lower bounds over these disjoint events and compare with probability one.</p>", "<p>Fix one cut, multiply conditional survival probabilities, then sum disjoint output probabilities over all distinct cuts. Check the graph and cut conventions.</p>", "<p>State the connected, positive-minimum-cut setting of the contraction argument. If disconnected zero cuts count as vertex partitions, the unrestricted claim fails; an edgeless graph has exponentially many such partitions.</p>"], "hw2026s1-p3#x": ["<p>Write an event for outputting each particular minimum cut. Ask whether a single run can output two different cuts.</p>", "<p>Karger's algorithm outputs any fixed minimum cut of a connected undirected multigraph with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Different output cuts are disjoint events. Keep parallel edges and discard self-loops during contraction.</p>", "<p>Use the per-cut contraction survival bound, then sum over the mutually exclusive outputs. Solve the resulting inequality for the number of cuts.</p>", "<p>Fix one cut, multiply conditional survival probabilities, then sum disjoint output probabilities over all distinct cuts. Check the graph and cut conventions.</p>", "<p>Use fixed-cut success, not success for any cut. Count unordered partitions once and retain the connectedness assumption.</p>"], "2018A-p1#e": ["<p>Separate two questions about contraction: does every run terminate quickly, and must every resulting cut be minimum?</p>", "<p>Monte Carlo algorithms have bounded runtime but may be wrong. Las Vegas algorithms are always correct but have random runtime, usually bounded in expectation. Timeouts and verification change these guarantees.</p>", "<p>Consider a run that contracts an edge of the minimum cut you hoped to preserve. A valid final cut need not be optimal, even though the prescribed contractions finish.</p>", "<p>Separate correctness from runtime, identify which can fail on an individual run, and match the actual guarantees to the definitions.</p>", "<p>Classify the stated algorithm, not a version with extra verification or indefinite repetition. A valid cut is not necessarily a correct minimum-cut answer.</p>"], "2025B-p1#c": ["<p>Treat the grade of a uniformly chosen student as a random variable. Translate ‘fraction of students’ into a probability.</p>", "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. Only nonnegativity and an expectation bound are needed; sometimes the useful variable counts failures rather than successes.</p>", "<p>Its expectation is the average grade. For positive average and nonnegative grades, compare the threshold with twice that expectation. Separately inspect the zero-average case if the statement permits it.</p>", "<p>Define the nonnegative variable, calculate its mean, translate the unwanted event, choose the threshold, apply Markov, and translate back.</p>", "<p>Markov requires a positive threshold. If all grades are zero and ‘at least twice the average’ is literal, everyone meets the threshold; mention this boundary case rather than dividing by zero.</p>"], "2023B-p6#x": ["<p>Give every element an independent random sign. For one fixed set, express its discrepancy as twice its positive-sign count minus its size.</p>", "<p>For independent indicators of common mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. A union bound adds failure probabilities for multiple requirements, even if those requirements are dependent.</p>", "<p>Apply a two-sided tail bound for that set, using its size or the upper bound from the statement. Make the individual failure probability small enough to sum over all sets.</p>", "<p>Fix one requirement, define its bad event, mean and deviation, and apply the bound. Count all requirements, union bound, choose constants, and analyse runtime.</p>", "<p>Did you distinguish the number of sets from the size of each set, keep both tails, and select a constant giving the exact required confidence?</p>"], "2024A-p2#x": ["<p>Sample uniformly random assignments, not uniformly random satisfying assignments. Define success by evaluating the entire CNF formula.</p>", "<p>Sample uniformly from the whole universe and test the property. The fraction of successful samples estimates the true fraction. Additive error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> with failure at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> follows from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> independent samples.</p>", "<p>Use accuracy one tenth and the exponentially small requested failure probability in the sample bound. This gives a polynomial sample count; each test must scan the formula.</p>", "<p>State the sampling space, success test and estimator. Substitute the requested accuracy and confidence, round samples up, and multiply by sampling and testing cost.</p>", "<p>Your runtime must depend on formula size as well as variable count. Returning a count instead of the fraction of successful trials would be the wrong estimator.</p>"], "2024C-p3#x": ["<p>Choose a uniformly random binary vector. For one fixed forbidden vector, define indicators for the coordinates where they differ.</p>", "<p>For independent indicators of common mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. A union bound adds failure probabilities for multiple requirements, even if those requirements are dependent.</p>", "<p>The distance is a sum of independent fair indicators with mean half the dimension. Translate distance below one third into a deviation from this mean, then union bound over the supplied set.</p>", "<p>Fix one requirement, define its bad event, mean and deviation, and apply the bound. Count all requirements, union bound, choose constants, and analyse runtime.</p>", "<p>Use the problem's bound on the number of forbidden vectors. Prove simultaneous distance from all of them, not merely large expected distance from one.</p>"], "2025B-p5#x": ["<p>Sample a uniformly random three-colouring of all vertices, and let success mean every edge is properly coloured.</p>", "<p>Sample uniformly from the whole universe and test the property. The fraction of successful samples estimates the true fraction. Additive error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> with failure at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> follows from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> independent samples.</p>", "<p>Substitute error equal to the reciprocal of the vertex count and the stated exponential failure probability. Generate each colouring and scan all edges to test it.</p>", "<p>State the sampling space, success test and estimator. Substitute the requested accuracy and confidence, round samples up, and multiply by sampling and testing cost.</p>", "<p>Include vertex generation and edge testing in runtime. This estimates the fraction of legal colourings; it is not an algorithm guaranteed to find one.</p>"], "2024B-p6#x": ["<p>Construct the random bipartite graph with the edge probability prescribed by the desired balance. Fix one pair of subsets and count edges between them.</p>", "<p>For independent indicators of common mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. A union bound adds failure probabilities for multiple requirements, even if those requirements are dependent.</p>", "<p>For this pair, independent edge indicators give a concentration bound in the product of the subset sizes. Count possible pairs of subsets of each size before selecting the logarithmic threshold constant.</p>", "<p>Fix one requirement, define its bad event, mean and deviation, and apply the bound. Count all requirements, union bound, choose constants, and analyse runtime.</p>", "<p>The union bound is over subset pairs, not just vertices. Check all allowed sizes and the relative-error versus additive-error conversion.</p>"], "2025A-p4#x": ["<p>Sample independent uniform sign vectors. Fix one nonempty coordinate subset from the definition of unbiasedness and inspect its parity or sign product on a single sample.</p>", "<p>For independent indicators of common mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. A union bound adds failure probabilities for multiple requirements, even if those requirements are dependent.</p>", "<p>Show that this product has mean zero, then concentrate its empirical average. There are exponentially many coordinate subsets, but only linearly many bits in their count's logarithm.</p>", "<p>Fix one requirement, define its bad event, mean and deviation, and apply the bound. Count all requirements, union bound, choose constants, and analyse runtime.</p>", "<p>Check whether the required output is a set or a sample multiset; duplicates require justification. Treat the empty coordinate subset separately, since its product is always one.</p>"], "hw2026s1-p6#x": ["<p>For one fixed nonempty test subset, write the sign product for a uniform random sampled vector. Why is it equally likely to be positive and negative?</p>", "<p>For independent indicators of common mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. A union bound adds failure probabilities for multiple requirements, even if those requirements are dependent.</p>", "<p>Convert sign products to fair indicators, concentrate their sample average, and union bound over all test subsets. Solve for a sample count giving failure at most one hundredth.</p>", "<p>Fix one requirement, define its bad event, mean and deviation, and apply the bound. Count all requirements, union bound, choose constants, and analyse runtime.</p>", "<p>Verify the precise balance normalization and set-versus-multiset convention. State construction time in terms of vector length and sample count.</p>"], "hw2025s1-p5#x": ["<p>Assign independent signs to elements, then focus on a single set's signed sum.</p>", "<p>For independent indicators of common mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. A union bound adds failure probabilities for multiple requirements, even if those requirements are dependent.</p>", "<p>Translate its absolute discrepancy into deviation of a binomial count from its mean. Make the per-set failure small enough for a union bound over the whole family.</p>", "<p>Fix one requirement, define its bad event, mean and deviation, and apply the bound. Count all requirements, union bound, choose constants, and analyse runtime.</p>", "<p>Name the random variables, their independence and the number of bad events. Overlap between sets does not invalidate the union bound.</p>"], "2014A-p8#b": ["<p>Use the number of unsatisfied clauses as your nonnegative variable. Translate satisfying fewer than three quarters into too many unsatisfied clauses.</p>", "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. Only nonnegativity and an expectation bound are needed; sometimes the useful variable counts failures rather than successes.</p>", "<p>Compute the expected unsatisfied count under the random assignment from part (a). Compare it with one quarter of all clauses using Markov.</p>", "<p>Define the nonnegative variable, calculate its mean, translate the unwanted event, choose the threshold, apply Markov, and translate back.</p>", "<p>Do not assume clause-satisfaction events are independent: clauses can share variables. Carefully preserve the strict or non-strict event inclusion.</p>"], "2015A-p5#x": ["<p>Write the geometric hit probability for a uniform sample from the specified cube. Express the desired estimate of pi as a constant times the empirical hit fraction.</p>", "<p>Sample uniformly from the whole universe and test the property. The fraction of successful samples estimates the true fraction. Additive error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> with failure at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> follows from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> independent samples.</p>", "<p>Convert the required relative accuracy for pi to additive accuracy for that hit probability before applying the concentration inequality. Use a justified bound if the resulting sample count would contain the unknown value.</p>", "<p>State the sampling space, success test and estimator. Substitute the requested accuracy and confidence, round samples up, and multiply by sampling and testing cost.</p>", "<p>Check the cube and ball dimensions, the scale factor, the confidence and integer rounding of sample size. Ten percent relative error is not automatically additive error one tenth.</p>"], "2015B-p5#x": ["<p>Let the original runtime be the random variable. Propose stopping after a constant multiple of its linear expectation bound.</p>", "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. Only nonnegativity and an expectation bound are needed; sometimes the useful variable counts failures rather than successes.</p>", "<p>Choose the timeout so Markov bounds the timeout probability by one tenth. Specify what the bounded-time algorithm outputs if the original has not finished.</p>", "<p>Define the nonnegative variable, calculate its mean, translate the unwanted event, choose the threshold, apply Markov, and translate back.</p>", "<p>Explain why a completed run is correct and only a timeout can cause error. The resulting runtime is worst-case linear, not merely expected linear.</p>"], "2017A-p4#x": ["<p>Each uniform random position gives an indicator for observing a one. The sample mean estimates the unknown fraction of ones.</p>", "<p>Sample uniformly from the whole universe and test the property. The fraction of successful samples estimates the true fraction. Additive error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> with failure at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> follows from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> independent samples.</p>", "<p>Use additive accuracy one hundredth and failure probability one tenth in the two-sided sample bound. Round up to an integer number of independent samples.</p>", "<p>State the sampling space, success test and estimator. Substitute the requested accuracy and confidence, round samples up, and multiply by sampling and testing cost.</p>", "<p>Specify sampling with replacement or justify the alternative concentration theorem. Check that the sample count targets the requested confidence, not its complement.</p>"], "2018B-p4#e": ["<p>Distinguish exact randomized selection from a capped attempt that may time out. A completed selection run is correct; the cap introduces the failure event.</p>", "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. Only nonnegativity and an expectation bound are needed; sometimes the useful variable counts failures rather than successes.</p>", "<p>Choose the stated cap so one trial times out with probability at most one half. Restart independently, up to five attempts, and multiply the timeout bounds.</p>", "<p>Define the nonnegative variable, calculate its mean, translate the unwanted event, choose the threshold, apply Markov, and translate back.</p>", "<p>If all five time out, the algorithm must report failure, not an unchecked answer. Five capped attempts give a failure-allowing bounded algorithm; indefinite retries of verified attempts give Las Vegas behaviour.</p>"], "2025A-p1#a": ["<p>Read ‘at most’ carefully. Let the score be the number of clauses satisfied by a uniform random assignment.</p>", "<p>On a finite probability space some outcome is at least the expectation, and some is at most it. This proves existence, not success on every random trial. Choose the direction matching the claim.</p>", "<p>Compute one clause's satisfaction probability under the stated distinct-literal assumptions. After finding the expected score, ask what would happen if every assignment scored strictly above it.</p>", "<p>Give a random construction, express its score using indicators, compute the expectation, and apply averaging in the requested direction.</p>", "<p>Use averaging in the ‘at most’ direction. Check the clause convention: tautologies or repeated literals can alter the usual three-distinct-variable probability.</p>"], "2017B-p4#a": ["<p>Try a uniform random assignment and one indicator per satisfied clause. You only need the weaker threshold requested, not an exact optimum.</p>", "<p>On a finite probability space some outcome is at least the expectation, and some is at most it. This proves existence, not success on every random trial. Choose the direction matching the claim.</p>", "<p>Bound the probability that a nonempty clause is satisfied from below by one half. Sum over clauses, then use existence of an outcome at least as good as the average.</p>", "<p>Give a random construction, express its score using indicators, compute the expectation, and apply averaging in the requested direction.</p>", "<p>Do not claim one assignment satisfies every clause or that clause indicators are independent. State the nonempty-clause assumption.</p>"], "2023B-p1#d": ["<p>Check what ‘second eigenvalue’ means in this course: second algebraically largest or second-largest absolute value. Also distinguish being at the target at the final time from visiting it by that time.</p>", "<p>A symmetric transition matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. In a regular graph, the constant vector has eigenvalue 1. Powers contract the orthogonal component according to the largest absolute nontrivial eigenvalue; negative eigenvalues matter too.</p>", "<p>Under an absolute-eigenvalue bound, subtract the uniform stationary vector and make its remaining norm smaller than half the reciprocal of the vertex count. If only an algebraic bound is given, a negative eigenvalue near minus one needs separate handling.</p>", "<p>Check normalization. Separate the constant component, bound remaining eigenvalues in absolute value, choose a power, and translate the coordinate bound into the requested probability.</p>", "<p>State exactly which eigenvalue and event the argument bounds. A mixing-time statement cannot silently ignore periodicity.</p>"], "2025A-p1#d": ["<p>Take an eigenvector with eigenvalue one and look at a coordinate where it is maximal.</p>", "<p>A symmetric transition matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. In a regular graph, the constant vector has eigenvalue 1. Powers contract the orthogonal component according to the largest absolute nontrivial eigenvalue; negative eigenvalues matter too.</p>", "<p>That coordinate equals the average of neighbouring coordinates. Equality at a maximum forces each neighbour to attain the same maximum; propagate along paths in the connected graph.</p>", "<p>Check normalization. Separate the constant component, bound remaining eigenvalues in absolute value, choose a power, and translate the coordinate bound into the requested probability.</p>", "<p>Show every coordinate is equal and that the constant vector really is an eigenvector. Connectedness is essential for the one-dimensional conclusion.</p>"], "2025B-p1#d": ["<p>Translate singularity of the shifted matrix into an eigenvalue statement for the original matrix. Check connectedness in the question before invoking a bipartiteness characterization.</p>", "<p>A symmetric transition matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. In a regular graph, the constant vector has eigenvalue 1. Powers contract the orthogonal component according to the largest absolute nontrivial eigenvalue; negative eigenvalues matter too.</p>", "<p>For a connected regular graph, use an eigenvector of eigenvalue minus one and equality in the neighbour-average bound to force alternating signs on edges. Without connectedness, one bipartite component only establishes the eigenvalue, not that every component is bipartite.</p>", "<p>Check normalization. Separate the constant component, bound remaining eigenvalues in absolute value, choose a power, and translate the coordinate bound into the requested probability.</p>", "<p>Include the graph's assumptions and normalization. If disconnected graphs are allowed, test a disjoint union of a regular bipartite component and a nonbipartite component of the same degree.</p>"], "2025C-p3#x": ["<p>Write the lazy transition matrix as one third identity plus two thirds the original transition matrix. What does this do to each eigenvalue?</p>", "<p>A symmetric transition matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. In a regular graph, the constant vector has eigenvalue 1. Powers contract the orthogonal component according to the largest absolute nontrivial eigenvalue; negative eigenvalues matter too.</p>", "<p>Bound the transformed negative eigenvalues away from minus one, and use the connected-graph spectral-gap estimate for the positive side. Choose a polynomial number of steps to make the error from uniform at most half the reciprocal of the vertex count.</p>", "<p>Check normalization. Separate the constant component, bound remaining eigenvalues in absolute value, choose a power, and translate the coordinate bound into the requested probability.</p>", "<p>Do not assume every lazy eigenvalue is nonnegative with this stay probability. Bound absolute values and distinguish ending at the target from hitting it earlier.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p1#a": ["<p>Write the sum of squared coordinates of the normalized eigenvector. For this part, you do not yet need the eigenvalue equation.</p>", "<p>A unit vector satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Orthogonality to the constant vector means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The first controls magnitudes; the second controls signs.</p>", "<p>Suppose every coordinate magnitude were smaller than the reciprocal square root of the number of coordinates. Sum their squared upper bounds.</p>", "<p>Write the relevant identity, assume no requested coordinate exists, and derive a contradiction. Distinguish a coordinate's magnitude from its sign.</p>", "<p>The conclusion is about absolute value, not positivity. A sign flip of the whole eigenvector is a separate WLOG step.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p1#b": ["<p>Use orthogonality to the constant vector, together with the positive coordinate provided by the preceding part.</p>", "<p>A unit vector satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Orthogonality to the constant vector means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The first controls magnitudes; the second controls signs.</p>", "<p>If all other coordinates were positive, or even all nonnegative, their sum could not cancel the given positive coordinate. Explain why the relevant eigenvectors are orthogonal.</p>", "<p>Write the relevant identity, assume no requested coordinate exists, and derive a contradiction. Distinguish a coordinate's magnitude from its sign.</p>", "<p>Justify the eigenvalue separation or connectedness fact needed for orthogonality; then explicitly identify the nonpositive coordinate.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p1#c": ["<p>Take a path between the large positive coordinate and the nonpositive coordinate from the previous parts.</p>", "<p>A connected graph has a simple path of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> edges between any two vertices. Endpoint coordinate differences telescope along it; the triangle inequality forces one sufficiently large edge difference.</p>", "<p>The endpoint difference is at least the reciprocal square root of the vertex count. If every edge difference were too small, the sum along a simple path would fall short of that difference.</p>", "<p>Choose the endpoints from earlier parts, take a simple path, telescope, apply the triangle inequality, divide by path length, and simplify.</p>", "<p>Use absolute differences and a simple path of bounded length. Do not assume coordinate values change monotonically along the path.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p1#d": ["<p>Start by expanding the squared differences in the requested identity. Keep the normalization of the lazy matrix visible.</p>", "<p>For symmetric stochastic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> and unit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>B</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Expand the square and use row and column sums of one. For an eigenvector, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>B</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> equals its eigenvalue.</p>", "<p>Replace the quadratic form by the eigenvalue, then retain the edge found in part (c) in the nonnegative sum. Substitute the lazy transition weight and square the coordinate-gap bound.</p>", "<p>Derive the identity, retain a nonnegative contribution from the large-difference edge, substitute its weight, and rearrange to a spectral-gap bound.</p>", "<p>Track the factor one half, the ordered-pair counting and the degree normalization. A stronger constant is fine only if all counting factors are justified.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p2#b": ["<p>Write each coordinate of the two set-indicator vectors and expand the matrix product as a double sum.</p>", "<p>Expand <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Indicator vectors restrict the sum to selected sets. Subtracting constant-vector components leaves the part bounded by nontrivial eigenvalues and Cauchy–Schwarz.</p>", "<p>Only pairs with first endpoint in the first set and second endpoint in the second set survive. Match each surviving adjacency entry with the ordered edge-count convention.</p>", "<p>Expand, match the edge-counting convention and normalization, then decompose vectors if needed. Bound the orthogonal contribution and restore the main term.</p>", "<p>Use the unnormalized adjacency matrix here. Edges inside the intersection may be counted twice; check that this agrees with the problem's definition.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p2#c": ["<p>Subtract each indicator vector's projection onto the constant vector. This separates the expected-density main term from the error term.</p>", "<p>Expand <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Indicator vectors restrict the sum to selected sets. Subtracting constant-vector components leaves the part bounded by nontrivial eigenvalues and Cauchy–Schwarz.</p>", "<p>The constant component contributes the degree times the product of set sizes divided by the vertex count. Bound the orthogonal bilinear term by the largest absolute nontrivial adjacency eigenvalue times the two vector norms.</p>", "<p>Expand, match the edge-counting convention and normalization, then decompose vectors if needed. Bound the orthogonal contribution and restore the main term.</p>", "<p>Do not mix normalized and unnormalized eigenvalues. Bound the residual norms by the square roots of the set sizes and retain an absolute value around the discrepancy.</p>"], "hw2025s2-p1#a": ["<p>Use the unit-length equation for the eigenvector. What is the average of its squared coordinates?</p>", "<p>A unit vector satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Orthogonality to the constant vector means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The first controls magnitudes; the second controls signs.</p>", "<p>Assume every squared coordinate is smaller than that average and sum over all coordinates to get a contradiction.</p>", "<p>Write the relevant identity, assume no requested coordinate exists, and derive a contradiction. Distinguish a coordinate's magnitude from its sign.</p>", "<p>State the squared-coordinate conclusion exactly before taking square roots. No independence or probability argument is involved.</p>"], "hw2025s2-p1#b": ["<p>Write the inner product with the all-ones vector as a coordinate sum. Recall the positive coordinate chosen in the previous step.</p>", "<p>A unit vector satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Orthogonality to the constant vector means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The first controls magnitudes; the second controls signs.</p>", "<p>A zero sum with at least one strictly positive term must contain a negative term, hence in particular a nonpositive one.</p>", "<p>Write the relevant identity, assume no requested coordinate exists, and derive a contradiction. Distinguish a coordinate's magnitude from its sign.</p>", "<p>Explain why the inner product is zero; symmetry and distinct eigenvalues, with the needed connectivity fact, provide this.</p>"], "hw2025s2-p1#c": ["<p>Join the positive and nonpositive coordinates by a simple path in the graph.</p>", "<p>A connected graph has a simple path of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> edges between any two vertices. Endpoint coordinate differences telescope along it; the triangle inequality forces one sufficiently large edge difference.</p>", "<p>Telescope the endpoint difference and compare it with the number of edges times the largest edge difference. Insert the lower bound from the preceding parts.</p>", "<p>Choose the endpoints from earlier parts, take a simple path, telescope, apply the triangle inequality, divide by path length, and simplify.</p>", "<p>Bound path length by the number of vertices and use absolute values. Cancellation along a path cannot invalidate the triangle-inequality bound.</p>"], "hw2025s2-p1#d": ["<p>Identify the stochastic matrix used in this version before substituting any edge weight.</p>", "<p>For symmetric stochastic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> and unit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>B</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Expand the square and use row and column sums of one. For an eigenvector, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>B</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> equals its eigenvalue.</p>", "<p>Use the squared-difference identity and keep the large-gap edge from part (c). Its positive transition weight converts the coordinate gap into a lower bound on one minus the eigenvalue.</p>", "<p>Derive the identity, retain a nonnegative contribution from the large-difference edge, substitute its weight, and rearrange to a spectral-gap bound.</p>", "<p>Check whether the matrix is lazy or not, and use its actual edge weight. Do not import a constant from the other homework version without checking.</p>"], "hw2025s2-p2#a": ["<p>Build a vector constant on one connected component and zero elsewhere. What does the normalized adjacency matrix do to it?</p>", "<p>A symmetric transition matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. In a regular graph, the constant vector has eigenvalue 1. Powers contract the orthogonal component according to the largest absolute nontrivial eigenvalue; negative eigenvalues matter too.</p>", "<p>Repeat for a second component. These component indicators are independent eigenvectors of eigenvalue one, which is also the largest possible eigenvalue.</p>", "<p>Check normalization. Separate the constant component, bound remaining eigenvalues in absolute value, choose a power, and translate the coordinate bound into the requested probability.</p>", "<p>Prove multiplicity at least two, not merely the existence of one eigenvector. State the ordering convention for the eigenvalues.</p>"], "hw2025s2-p2#b": ["<p>Assign coordinate plus one to one bipartition side and minus one to the other.</p>", "<p>A symmetric transition matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. In a regular graph, the constant vector has eigenvalue 1. Powers contract the orthogonal component according to the largest absolute nontrivial eigenvalue; negative eigenvalues matter too.</p>", "<p>Every neighbour has the opposite sign, so the normalized neighbour average negates the coordinate. Then use the spectral range of a symmetric transition matrix.</p>", "<p>Check normalization. Separate the constant component, bound remaining eigenvalues in absolute value, choose a power, and translate the coordinate bound into the requested probability.</p>", "<p>Show the vector is nonzero and check normalization. Establish that minus one is the smallest eigenvalue, not just an eigenvalue.</p>"], "2017B-p3#x": ["<p>Let the cover time be the random variable and relate ‘target not yet visited’ to ‘not all vertices visited’.</p>", "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. Only nonnegativity and an expectation bound are needed; sometimes the useful variable counts failures rather than successes.</p>", "<p>Apply the given expectation bound at the stated time horizon. The target's hitting time is bounded by the cover time, so the cover-time tail bound suffices.</p>", "<p>Define the nonnegative variable, calculate its mean, translate the unwanted event, choose the threshold, apply Markov, and translate back.</p>", "<p>Use a subset relation between failure events, not equality. Substitute the exact horizon and take the complement for the requested success probability.</p>"], "2023A-p1#b": ["<p>Revisit the isolation proof with all weights except one fixed. Does a product-weight tie still forbid at most one value for the remaining weight?</p>", "<p>For a nonempty family of subsets of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>-element ground set, independent uniform weights in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> give a unique minimum-weight member with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>. Member weight normally means the sum of element weights.</p>", "<p>Separate sets containing the chosen element from those not containing it. Positive fixed weights turn their best products into one quantity linear in the remaining weight and one constant quantity. Count possible bad values and union bound.</p>", "<p>Identify the ground set and nonempty family. Apply isolation to each requirement, union bound, choose the weight range, and bound bit lengths and runtime.</p>", "<p>Do not quote the sum-version lemma unchanged. Explain positivity, the at-most-one tie value and why multiple minima force a bad element.</p>"], "2024A-p3#x": ["<p>For one vertex pair, view simple paths as a family of edge subsets. Positive weights ensure a minimum walk can be taken simple.</p>", "<p>For a nonempty family of subsets of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>-element ground set, independent uniform weights in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> give a unique minimum-weight member with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>. Member weight normally means the sum of element weights.</p>", "<p>Use the number of edges as the ground-set size and union bound the isolation failure over all relevant vertex pairs. Choose a polynomial weight range making the total failure at most one tenth.</p>", "<p>Identify the ground set and nonempty family. Apply isolation to each requirement, union bound, choose the weight range, and bound bit lengths and runtime.</p>", "<p>Treat pairs with no connecting path according to the question's assumptions. Explain that one shared weight assignment must work simultaneously for all pairs.</p>"], "2025C-p1#c": ["<p>There are two uniqueness requirements: a minimum cut and a maximum cut. A single application of minimum-isolation addresses only one.</p>", "<p>For a nonempty family of subsets of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>-element ground set, independent uniform weights in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> give a unique minimum-weight member with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>. Member weight normally means the sum of element weights.</p>", "<p>For maximum weight, apply minimum-isolation to complements of cut edge sets within the full edge set. The total edge weight is common to all cuts. Union bound the two failure events with the given range.</p>", "<p>Identify the ground set and nonempty family. Apply isolation to each requirement, union bound, choose the weight range, and bound bit lengths and runtime.</p>", "<p>No independence between the two uniqueness events is needed. Check whether cuts are identified by edge sets or vertex partitions, especially for disconnected graphs.</p>"], "hw2026s2-p3#x": ["<p>Use Cauchy–Binet to express a determinant whose terms select equally indexed column bases in both matrices.</p>", "<p>A determinant is a signed sum of products. Variables can encode combinatorial objects as monomials, but cancellation must be addressed. A nonzero total-degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial vanishes at independent uniform coordinates in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Assign isolating weights to column indices so a unique minimum-weight common basis gives a unique lowest-degree nonzero term. Use the symbolic determinant for detection, then explain how to recover actual columns if required.</p>", "<p>Define the polynomial, identify its terms and justify noncancellation. Bound degree and sampling range. Explain evaluation, recovery if required, error direction and runtime.</p>", "<p>Show what happens when there is no common basis, why the isolated coefficient cannot cancel, and why polynomial weights keep the algebraic representation manageable.</p>"], "hw2025s2-p3#x": ["<p>Introduce a variable tracking the number of red edges in a matching. The coefficient at the requested red count is the quantity to inspect.</p>", "<p>A determinant is a signed sum of products. Variables can encode combinatorial objects as monomials, but cancellation must be addressed. A nonzero total-degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial vanishes at independent uniform coordinates in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>", "<p>Use isolation within the family of matchings having exactly the required red count. Explain how the prescribed powers of two distinguish the isolated minimum term despite determinant signs, and how matching edges are recovered.</p>", "<p>Define the polynomial, identify its terms and justify noncancellation. Bound degree and sampling range. Explain evaluation, recovery if required, error direction and runtime.</p>", "<p>Track bit lengths as well as arithmetic operations. If extracting a matching, justify each self-reduction query and account for all failure probabilities.</p>"]};
